    "id": "apm-ai-15",
    "q": "When should you use a foundation model vs. a fine-tuned model?",
    "a": "Default to foundation models first; fine-tune only when you've proven a specific, measurable gap.\n\nDecision framework:\n1. Prototype with foundation model + prompt engineering\n2. Validate on 100+ real user examples, measuring task-specific accuracy, latency, and cost\n3. If foundation model meets quality threshold → ship it\n4. If it fails in consistent, diagnosable patterns → fine-tuning becomes justified\n\nWhen fine-tuning earns consideration:\n- Foundation model accuracy plateaus below acceptable threshold (e.g., <85% on your eval set)\n- Domain requires specialized vocabulary prompting can't reliably inject (legal, clinical, financial)\n- Latency or cost constraints require a smaller, optimized model\n- You have 500+ high-quality labeled examples ready\n\nPilot structure:\nRun a 2-week A/B test: 50% traffic to foundation model, 50% to fine-tuned variant. Measure task accuracy on held-out eval set, P95 latency, cost-per-query, and user satisfaction score. Require statistical significance before switching.\n\nKey metrics: accuracy delta, cost reduction percentage, latency improvement, error rate on out-of-distribution inputs.\n\nCritical trade-off: Fine-tuning improves in-distribution performance but degrades generalization. A fine-tuned model trained on customer support tickets may fail on slightly novel queries. Always test on diverse, out-of-distribution examples before deploying.\n\nBottom line: Fine-tuning is an optimization, not a starting point. Most teams reach production value faster by shipping foundation models and fine-tuning only after real failure patterns emerge from production data.",
