    "id": "apm-ai-16",
    "q": "How do you handle a situation where your AI feature performs well on average but fails for specific user groups?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Detect → Classify → Act → Measure\n\nWhen average metrics mask group-level failures, I treat it as a product integrity issue, not just a model bug.\n\n1. Validate the gap first\nSegment performance by demographics, language, device, tenure, and use case. Run stratified evaluation on held-out data before drawing conclusions. Pair quantitative gaps with qualitative interviews from affected users to understand *why* the failure occurs-data drift, training underrepresentation, or feature mismatch.\n\n2. Classify severity\nDoes this touch a protected characteristic? If yes, legal exposure and urgency escalate immediately. Is the feature unusable for this group, or just degraded? That determines response speed.\n\n3. Act proportionally\n- High severity: Restrict feature access for affected groups until fixed; communicate proactively\n- Medium: Run a targeted A/B test-serve the current model to 50% of the affected group, a fine-tuned variant (trained on augmented representative data) to the other 50%\n- Low: Add honest disclaimers while collecting targeted feedback data with consent\n\n4. Measure success explicitly\n- Group-specific task completion rate\n- Error rate parity across segments (target <15% relative gap)\n- Retention delta for affected users post-fix\n\nTrade-off awareness: Restricting the feature protects users but reduces engagement metrics short-term. Fine-tuning on a small subgroup risks overfitting. I'd document both risks in the ML roadmap, not the bug queue, and set a 60-day remediation SLA to force prioritization.",
