    "id": "apm-ai-18",
    "q": "How do you decide when automation should replace a human step vs. when humans should stay in the loop?",
    "a": "Framework: Automate by consequence × confidence, validated by data-not optimism.\n\nI evaluate three axes: error consequence (reversible vs. irreversible), AI accuracy vs. a defined threshold, and user trust maturity.\n\nKeep humans in the loop when: errors are high-stakes (financial, medical, legal), accuracy falls below a validated threshold, or the AI lacks context like emotional nuance or organizational dynamics. Also when we're early in the product lifecycle and trust is unproven.\n\nMove toward automation when: errors are low-consequence and easily correctable, accuracy is consistently high on held-out test sets, human review creates friction that meaningfully degrades experience, or volume makes review unsustainable.\n\nThe gradient, not the binary:\n1. AI suggests → human approves\n2. AI acts → human can undo\n3. AI acts → exceptions flagged\n4. AI acts → audit log only\n\nValidation step before moving stages: I require real-world error rate data on 1,000+ cases, not just test set performance, before advancing.\n\nSpecific pilot: For email drafting, run an A/B test-Variant A: AI drafts, user reviews. Variant B: AI sends automatically with undo. Gate promotion to Variant B only if edit/override rate drops below 10% for 30 consecutive days across a statistically significant cohort.\n\nSuccess metrics: override rate, error rate, task completion time, user trust score (surveyed), and escalation rate.\n\nCore trade-off: Speed and scale favor full automation; trust, safety, and regulatory compliance favor human oversight. Move the gradient based on evidence, not product pressure.",
