    "id": "apm-ai-20",
    "q": "How do you think about user expectations when launching an AI feature for the first time?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Calibrated Optimism at Launch\n\nUser expectations for AI features cluster into two failure modes: \"magic that never fails\" and \"AI never works for me.\" Both destroy retention. My goal is calibrated optimism-communicating specifically what the feature does well and where human review matters.\n\nBefore writing a line of copy, I validate:\n- User mental models via 5-8 discovery interviews\n- Baseline task success rate and where the model actually underperforms\n- Competitor expectation-setting patterns\n\nDesign interventions, prioritized:\n1. Onboarding disclosure: One screen, specific accuracy claim (\"accurate ~85% of the time\"), editable outputs emphasized\n2. Confidence signals: Surface high/low confidence inline-don't homogenize outputs\n3. Graceful uncertainty: Explicit \"I'm not confident here-please verify\" beats silent errors\n4. Win-first flow: Route first-time users toward tasks with highest model accuracy\n\nPilot structure:\nA/B test three onboarding variants-no disclosure, generic disclosure, specific accuracy disclosure-measuring 7-day repeat use rate, override rate, and trust score (post-use survey). I'd expect specific disclosure to outperform generic despite feeling riskier.\n\nKey trade-off:\nTransparency about limitations may suppress initial adoption but protects long-term trust. Overpromising inflates Day-1 activation while accelerating churn after first failure.\n\nTrust deficits compound. Getting calibration right at launch is 10x cheaper than rebuilding after a public mistake.",
