    "id": "apm-ai-24",
    "q": "How would you think about pricing a new AI feature you are adding to an existing free-tier product?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Gate by value type, validate by unit economics, test by cohort.\n\nAI features differ from standard features because every user action has a marginal cost. That changes free-tier math fundamentally.\n\nStep 1 - Classify the feature. Is AI core to the product working (absorb cost, optimize over time) or an accelerator for power users (gate behind paid)? This determines your starting posture.\n\nStep 2 - Validate unit economics before launch. Model the median free user: if they trigger 10 AI calls/month at $0.05 each, that's $0.50/month unmonetized. At 10,000 free users, $5K/month in unrecovered cost. Your conversion rate must offset this or the feature needs a cap. Run this model against your actual DAU and usage distribution-not averages, use P75 users who drive disproportionate cost.\n\nStep 3 - Pilot with a usage-cap A/B test. Split free users into three cohorts: unlimited, 10 actions/month, 5 actions/month. Measure upgrade conversion rate, feature retention, and churn delta over 30 days. This reveals price sensitivity without committing to a pricing structure.\n\nKey metrics: upgrade conversion rate, cost-per-free-user, AI feature activation rate, and 30-day retention by cohort.\n\nCore trade-off: Caps drive revenue but risk reducing the viral, habit-forming usage that makes the paid tier desirable in the first place. Avoid per-action pricing early-cost unpredictability suppresses conversion more than caps do.",
