    "id": "apm-ai-26",
    "q": "Your AI feature needs 3 sprints of data pipeline work before any user-facing value. How do you keep stakeholders engaged?",
    "a": "Framework: Show Progress, Ship Parallel, Set Explicit Milestones\n\nFirst, I'd validate the assumption: can we truly ship nothing user-facing in sprints 1-2? Often a rule-based fallback or lightweight data visualization can ship earlier. If confirmed, here's my approach.\n\nThree-part strategy:\n\n1. Make infrastructure visible\nPublish an explicit roadmap: \"Sprints 1-3: data pipeline. Sprint 4: AI feature launch.\" At each sprint review, demo measurable internal progress-records processed, data quality score, model accuracy baseline. Vague timelines destroy trust; concrete numbers rebuild it.\n\n2. Ship a parallel MVP\nIn sprint 2, launch a heuristic-based version of the feature. Lower quality, but real user value. Frame it publicly: \"We're shipping rule-based recommendations now, upgrading to ML-powered recommendations in sprint 4 with projected 35-40% accuracy improvement.\" This maintains shipping discipline while infrastructure builds.\n\n3. Create stakeholder investment\nInvite 3-5 key customers into a beta program. Show them the data being collected, the problem being solved. Customers who feel involved wait longer and advocate harder.\n\nSuccess metrics: stakeholder escalation frequency (target: zero mid-sprint interruptions), parallel MVP retention rate, model accuracy at sprint 3 gate (define the threshold before starting).\n\nKey trade-offs:\n- Parallel MVP risks anchoring customers on the inferior version-mitigate with clear versioning messaging\n- Internal demos risk eroding confidence if metrics regress-only demo when you can defend the numbers\n\nValidation: if stakeholder questions decrease sprint-over-sprint, engagement is holding.",
