    "id": "apm-ai-27",
    "q": "How do you write user stories for AI features where the output is non-deterministic?",
    "a": "Framework: Outcome-Based Stories with Probabilistic Acceptance Criteria\n\nFor AI features with non-deterministic outputs, replace correctness-based criteria with usefulness thresholds validated through real user behavior-not QA alone.\n\n--\n\nBefore writing the story, validate three assumptions:\n- What does \"good output\" look like to the user? (interviews, not guesses)\n- What's the baseline without AI? (measure current time/effort first)\n- Is regulatory determinism required? (if yes, AI may be wrong feature entirely)\n\n--\n\nExample: AI email draft for product managers\n\nContext: PMs spend 30 minutes writing routine update emails. Goal is saving 15+ minutes per draft, not perfection.\n\nProbabilistic Acceptance Criteria:\n- Draft generates within 5 seconds\n- User edits the draft rather than rewrites it (edit rate ≥70%)\n- Average edit time <5 minutes\n- Zero factually false product claims (validated via 100-sample human review weekly)\n\nPilot: 2-week A/B test with 50 early users-AI draft vs. writing from scratch. Primary metric: edit rate. Secondary metrics: time-to-send, user satisfaction score (CSAT ≥4/5), rewrite rate.\n\nShip threshold: Edit rate >70% AND average edit time <5 minutes after Week 2.\n\n--\n\nTrade-offs to surface explicitly:\n- Probabilistic criteria require real users, not QA-slower validation cycles\n- High edit rates signal usefulness, but don't catch subtle brand-voice drift; requires ongoing human review\n- Setting threshold too low (≥50%) ships mediocre output; too high (≥90%) delays indefinitely\n\nDefinition of done shifts from \"always correct\" to \"reliably useful enough to prefer over starting from scratch.\"",
