    "id": "apm-ai-28",
    "q": "Design a sprint structure for a team building both traditional product features and AI/ML capabilities.",
    "a": "Sprint Structure for Hybrid Feature/AI Teams\n\nRecommendation: Run dual-track sprints with asymmetric commitment models within a single two-week cadence.\n\nFirst, validate the problem: Before designing structure, instrument your current sprints. If AI work causes >20% of sprint commitments to slip, you have a structural mismatch. If miss rate is <10%, your problem is process, not structure.\n\nThe framework - two commitment classes:\n\n- Feature track (committed): Traditional scrum with hard DoD. Features ship against existing or baseline models. Target: 90%+ sprint completion rate.\n- AI track (time-boxed): Fixed hours, not fixed outcomes. \"Spend 40 hours improving recommendation precision; report results Friday.\" Target: experiment velocity, not model performance.\n\nDecouple by design: Feature teams build against mock services or baseline models. AI teams iterate in parallel. Integration happens at sprint end - if the new model isn't ready, the feature ships with baseline anyway.\n\nPilot: Run this structure for three sprints. Measure feature completion rate, AI experiment velocity, and integration blockers (target: zero AI-caused feature delays).\n\nSuccess metrics: Sprint commitment rate (features), experiments completed per sprint (AI), and percentage of features shipping without AI blocking.\n\nKey trade-offs:\n- Decoupling enables velocity but may ship features with suboptimal models temporarily\n- Separate commitment models reduce pressure on AI teams but require strong communication to prevent misalignment on quality expectations\n\nShift capacity ratios sprint-by-sprint based on dependencies, not habit.",
