    "q": "How would you approach go-to-market strategy for a B2C app?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Nail It Before You Scale It - Segment → Acquire → Retain → Scale\n\nBefore committing to channels, I'd validate three assumptions: Is the product standalone or network-effect-driven? Do we have existing distribution or starting cold? Are we targeting early adopters or mainstream users? I'll assume standalone, no existing channels, mainstream B2C audience.\n\nStep 1: Hyper-specific segmentation. Not \"health-conscious users\" - \"women 28-38 who've abandoned 3+ fitness apps because they couldn't fit them into a packed schedule.\" Specificity drives channel selection and creative.\n\nStep 2: Choose one primary growth model to prove first. Paid (Meta/TikTok), organic/SEO, referral, or influencer/community. For mainstream B2C, I'd pilot paid acquisition on a $5K test budget against two audience segments, measuring install rate, activation rate, and CAC within two weeks before committing budget.\n\nStep 3: Fix the funnel before scaling spend. A leaky funnel makes paid acquisition expensive fast. Gate scaled acquisition behind hitting: activation rate >40%, D7 retention >25%, CAC payback <6 months.\n\nStep 4: Concentrated launch moment. Product Hunt, influencer seeding, and press in a tight window generates compounding signal.\n\nTrade-offs: Paid scales fast but dies if unit economics slip. SEO is durable but slow. Referral requires inherent shareability - forced mechanics backfire.\n\nSuccess metrics: Activation rate, D7/D30 retention, CAC, LTV:CAC ratio, and payback period.",
