    "q": "How do you balance user feedback with product vision?",
    "a": "Framework: Vision sets direction; feedback calibrates the path. Neither overrides the other-conflicts become hypotheses to test.\n\nFirst, I validate whether feedback is signal or noise: Is it coming from target users or vocal outliers? Is it pointing to a problem or prescribing a solution? \"I can't find my files\" is a problem signal-high credibility. \"Add folders\" is a solution prescription-requires skepticism.\n\nMy four-step approach:\n\n1. Separate problem feedback from solution feedback. Weight problem reports heavily; treat solution requests as hypotheses.\n2. Segment by user type. Target users get high weight; tangential or churned users get low weight regardless of volume or intensity.\n3. Stress-test vision assumptions. If feedback repeatedly contradicts a core assumption, that assumption gets examined-not rationalized away. Persistent contradiction is a vision liability.\n4. Run a structured pilot. When conflict is genuine, I form an explicit hypothesis, design a 2-week A/B test or constrained rollout, and measure retention delta, task completion rate, and NPS by segment-not just engagement volume.\n\nTrade-offs I hold: Moving too far toward feedback produces incoherent feature lists shaped by the loudest minority. Moving too far toward vision risks founder delusion-building confidently toward something users don't value.\n\nSuccess looks like: target-segment retention improves, support tickets on the pain point drop, and the decision either reinforces or meaningfully updates the vision with evidence-not gut feel.",
