    "q": "How would you design a dashboard feature for small business owners who are not data-literate?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Recommendation: Design for decisions, not data display.\n\nBefore building anything, I'd validate the core assumption that small business owners lack the right *format*, not the right *data*. I'd interview 12 owners across retail, services, and e-commerce, asking: \"What decision did you make yesterday, and what information drove it?\" This separates genuine decision-support needs from feature requests.\n\nHypothesis: Most small business owners need fewer, clearer signals-not more charts. \"Your best revenue week in 2 months\" outperforms a trend line they can't interpret.\n\nDesign principles:\n- One metric per card, in plain language with context: \"47 orders this week-8 more than last week\"\n- Trend narratives over percentages: \"Sales are slowing-Tuesday is typically your strongest day\"\n- Actionable prompts: \"Product X has 3 days of inventory left-reorder?\"\n- Mobile-first, zero-configuration defaults\n\nPilot: Run an A/B test on 500 existing users-current chart-heavy dashboard vs. the narrative version. Primary metric: decision-action rate (users who view dashboard and take a platform action within the same session). Secondary metrics: DAU retention at day-7 and day-30, support ticket volume related to dashboard confusion, and session time (lower can indicate clarity, not disengagement).\n\nTrade-offs: Plain language reduces flexibility for data-literate users who want drill-downs. I'd solve this with a \"simple/advanced\" toggle rather than building two separate products, preserving both segments without doubling development cost.",
