    "q": "How do you validate product-market fit signals before committing resources to a roadmap direction?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Before committing roadmap resources, I use a four-layer validation framework that stress-tests signals rather than confirms them-because mistaking early enthusiasm for durable fit is the costliest PM error.\n\nLayer 1: Retention as ground truth. Flat retention curves (consistent cohort return rates month-over-month) outweigh any survey metric. I target >40% on the Sean Ellis \"very disappointed\" test as a directional signal only-never as the decision gate.\n\nLayer 2: Unsolicited organic behavior. Unprompted referrals, user-built integrations, and angry complaints when features change are stronger PMF indicators than any prompted feedback. Behavior precedes stated preference.\n\nLayer 3: Power user forensics. I identify the top 10% of engaged users and reverse-engineer their Week 1 behavior patterns. This defines the \"aha moment\" I need to systematize across the broader funnel.\n\nLayer 4: Counterfactual and willingness-to-pay tests. I ask directly: \"If this disappeared tomorrow, what would you do?\" Users with concrete alternatives confirm real embedding. I also run explicit pricing experiments before assuming revenue PMF-enthusiasm without payment tolerance doesn't support unit economics.\n\nData validation: Before any roadmap commitment, I require 90-day retention data across at least two distinct user cohorts.\n\nPilot structure: I run a controlled A/B test on onboarding flows designed to replicate power-user Week 1 behavior, measuring 30/60/90-day retention lift, NPS delta, and conversion-to-paid rate.\n\nTrade-off awareness: This framework adds 6–8 weeks before full resource commitment-worth it for irreversible roadmap bets, potentially too slow for fast-moving competitive windows.",
