    "q": "How would you design a product that helps freelancers manage invoicing and payments?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Design around the core job-\"get paid without spending emotional or administrative energy on it.\"\n\nValidate first. Before building, interview 15–20 freelancers across design, writing, development, and consulting. Key questions: How long does invoicing take weekly? How often do clients pay late? What triggers the most anxiety? This surfaces whether friction is primarily *creation*, *collection*, or *follow-up*-each implies a different V1 priority.\n\nV1 feature set (deliberately narrow):\n- Invoice generation from a time log or project-not a blank form\n- Embedded \"Pay Now\" button in email; two-click payment for clients\n- Automated reminders at configurable intervals (3 days before due, day-of, 3 and 7 days after)-eliminates emotional labor of chasing\n- Three dashboard numbers: received, outstanding, overdue\n\nDeliberately excluded: time tracking, contracts, tax calculation, project management integrations. Real needs-but they dilute the core job in V1.\n\nPilot: Launch with 200 freelancers across two cohorts. A/B test automated reminder cadence (aggressive vs. gentle tone) to measure which drives faster payment without damaging client relationships.\n\nSuccess metrics: on-time payment rate (target: >60% vs. ~40% industry baseline), invoice creation time (<3 minutes), and 30-day retention.\n\nPricing trade-off: Transaction fees (1–2.9%) align incentives and lower adoption friction but hurt trust at scale. Offer flat subscription for high-volume users (>$10K/month invoiced) to prevent churn to competitors like HoneyBook.\n\nCore trade-off to watch: Automation that feels too aggressive risks damaging freelancer-client relationships-the product's primary asset.",
