    "q": "How would you approach reducing customer support volume by 30% through product improvements?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Diagnose → Intervene → Measure → Scale\n\nBefore building anything, I'd validate the problem with data. I'd pull 6 months of support tickets, categorize by root cause, and identify the top 5 issue types by volume. In most products, 20% of issue types drive 80% of tickets. That's where I focus.\n\nTypical categories and targeted interventions:\n\n- Can't find existing features → Improve navigation IA, add contextual tooltips at empty states, build in-product \"how do I...\" search indexed to features\n- Confused how something works → Contextual inline help (not help center links), redesigned empty states, simplified UI-repeated tickets about the same element are a UX defect, not a training problem\n- Something broken → Escalate as a product defect; this isn't a content problem\n\nPilot design: I'd run a 30-day A/B test on the highest-volume category-say, \"feature discoverability\"-exposing 50% of users to improved empty states and in-product search.\n\nSuccess metrics: Ticket volume per category (primary), deflection rate from in-product help, CSAT on support interactions, and time-to-resolution for remaining tickets.\n\nTrade-offs to acknowledge: Over-investing in self-serve help can mask deeper UX problems. If ticket volume drops but CSAT doesn't improve, we've optimized the wrong thing. I'd also build a feedback loop-weekly support categorization reviewed in PM standup-to sustain the 30% reduction rather than achieve it once.",
