    "q": "How would you approach building a mobile-first version of a product that currently only exists as a desktop SaaS?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Identify → Validate → Pilot → Measure\n\nMobile-first means reimagining which use cases belong on mobile, not shrinking the desktop product.\n\nStep 1: Identify mobile-native use cases\nAudit desktop session data by task duration and frequency. Features used in short bursts-approvals, status checks, notifications-are mobile candidates. Complex, extended workflows (report building, document editing) stay desktop-native in V1. This preserves scope and focuses investment.\n\nStep 2: Validate before building\nBefore any development, run discovery interviews with 15-20 users. Hypothesis to test: \"Users abandon time-sensitive tasks because they require a desktop.\" Measure current mobile visit rate via analytics-if it's below 10%, mobile may not warrant native investment yet.\n\nStep 3: Pilot with a focused A/B test\nBuild a single high-frequency workflow (e.g., approval flow) as a mobile-native experience. Run it against a responsive fallback with 50/50 traffic split over 6 weeks.\n\nSuccess metrics:\n- Task completion rate (mobile native vs. responsive)\n- D7 and D30 retention for mobile-first users\n- Weekly active usage rate (target: >40% of mobile installs)\n- Time-to-complete for core workflow\n\nStep 4: Architecture decision\nNative app wins when mobile becomes a primary surface. Responsive is acceptable for secondary B2B use cases-lower cost, faster iteration, but compromised experience.\n\nTrade-off: Native delivers better retention but doubles engineering cost and maintenance burden. Start native only where data confirms mobile is a primary job-to-be-done.",
