    "q": "How do you prioritize between improving the core product experience versus expanding to adjacent use cases?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "I treat this as a portfolio allocation decision driven by core product health metrics, not intuition.\n\nMy framework:\n\n1. Diagnose core health first. I won't greenlight adjacent expansion if core metrics show structural weakness: D30 retention below category benchmark, NPS under 40, or churn concentrated in a specific friction point. Expansion amplifies what already exists-if the core leaks, expansion accelerates user loss, not growth.\n\n2. Validate adjacent demand before committing. Before any build, I run a two-week discovery sprint: landing page tests, customer interviews, and activation rate proxies. This surfaces whether adjacent use cases genuinely reinforce the core loop or create a disconnected product-within-a-product.\n\n3. Run an explicit opportunity cost model. I force the comparison into numbers: \"Investing one quarter of engineering in core reduces churn by an estimated 8%, worth $400K ARR retained. Adjacent expansion targets a new segment worth $600K if conversion holds.\" This surfaces the real tradeoff rather than letting it remain abstract.\n\n4. Stage adjacent bets with kill criteria. Start with a time-boxed pilot-4 weeks, minimal scope. Define success thresholds upfront: ≥15% feature adoption among target users, no measurable degradation in core engagement metrics.\n\nKey tradeoff I flag explicitly: Adjacent expansion that succeeds short-term but raises product complexity can permanently increase onboarding friction and support costs-a delayed tax on core retention. I weight that risk explicitly in prioritization.",
