    "q": "How do you work with a sales team that keeps making product promises you can't keep?",
    "a": "This is a process failure with a cultural root cause. Before intervening, I'd validate the scope: pull CRM data to identify how frequently false commitments appear in closed deals, which reps are responsible, and whether churned accounts correlate with undelivered promises. That diagnosis shapes the response.\n\nThree structured interventions:\n\n1. Two-tier roadmap visibility. Publish a \"committed vs. exploratory\" reference for sales. Committed items have ship dates; exploratory items require PM sign-off before becoming deal commitments. This removes ambiguity without removing sales flexibility.\n\n2. Deal commitment gate. Any off-roadmap promise requires a structured request: deal size, competitive necessity, required timeline. PM responds within 48 hours. This replaces informal hallway handshakes with a formal, auditable process.\n\n3. Incentive realignment. Partner with the sales leader to flag deals closed on false commitments that churn within 90 days. Not punitive-just visible. Short-term quota pressure disappears; deal quality becomes a shared metric.\n\nPilot: Run this with one sales pod for one quarter before scaling. Measure: reduction in unvetted feature commitments, 90-day retention on affected accounts, and PM time spent firefighting customer escalations.\n\nTrade-offs to acknowledge: The 48-hour gate adds friction that could slow deal velocity. To mitigate, I'd hold weekly office hours with sales so low-urgency questions don't pile up as formal requests.\n\nThe relationship layer matters too-meeting individually with the top five reps surfaces real competitive gaps versus narrative problems, and builds the trust that makes process compliance sustainable.",
