    "q": "How would you design a search experience for a job platform from the candidate's perspective?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Design for fit confidence, not just result volume.\n\nCandidates fail at job search in three ways: irrelevant results, no signal on competitiveness, and missed opportunities. I'd prioritize in that order.\n\nBefore building, validate assumptions. Run session recordings and exit surveys to confirm whether drop-off happens at search results, job detail pages, or application start. If candidates abandon at results, it's a relevance problem; if at detail pages, it's a fit-signal problem.\n\nCore design decisions:\n- Profile-weighted ranking for logged-in users (skills, experience level, past applications) over pure keyword match\n- Fit indicators on every result card: matched vs. missing skills, plus \"You're in the top 25% of applicants by skill match\"\n- Filters that reduce noise meaningfully: \"Meets 80%+ requirements,\" \"Posted within 14 days,\" \"Visa sponsorship available\"\n\nPilot: A/B test fit indicators on result cards against control (no indicators). Hypothesis: candidates with fit signals apply to fewer but better-matched roles.\n\nSuccess metrics: Interview conversion rate (primary), application completion rate (started → submitted), and search-to-apply rate. Deliberately avoid optimizing for raw application volume-that harms both candidates and employers.\n\nKey trade-off: Showing competitive context (\"73 applicants\") may discourage qualified candidates from applying to competitive roles. Monitor drop-off by applicant quality tier to catch false negatives before full rollout.",
