    "q": "How do you think about your own career development as a PM?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "I structure PM career development across three dimensions-craft, scope, and influence-and treat each like a product problem: diagnose gaps with data, run experiments, measure outcomes.\n\nThe framework:\n- Craft: research, strategy, prioritization, writing, data analysis\n- Scope: complexity of system owned-feature → product → platform → portfolio\n- Influence: executing others' roadmaps → defining strategy → shaping company direction\n\nThe critical insight is sequencing: expanding scope without mastered craft is how promising PMs fail. Influence is downstream of credibility, not title.\n\nHow I apply this personally:\nEach quarter I identify one specific capability gap, find a proxy metric for it, and design a deliberate practice. For example, if improving data fluency, I track whether I can independently answer product questions without analyst support within 48 hours-baseline it, then measure progress at 90 days.\n\nValidation step: I pressure-test my self-assessment against external signals-360 feedback, skip-level conversations, peer benchmarking against PMs at my level in top companies. Self-perception of gaps is notoriously unreliable.\n\nThe trade-off I'm explicit about: Deep craft development sometimes slows visible scope expansion, which can look like stagnation. That's a career risk worth accepting short-term to avoid the more catastrophic risk of over-leveling into complexity you can't execute against.\n\nThe fastest-growing PMs I've observed share one trait: genuine discomfort with their current skill ceiling, not satisfaction with competence.",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "domain": "general",
