    "q": "Tell me about a time you had to reprioritize mid-sprint and how you handled it.",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "When mid-sprint reprioritization hits, my framework is: validate severity, minimize blast radius, communicate with specificity, and capture the learning.\n\nTwo days into a checkout redesign sprint, our security team flagged a live vulnerability in our payment processing flow affecting all active transactions. P0, no debate.\n\nValidate first: Before pulling anyone off the redesign, I spent 20 minutes with the security engineer confirming actual exposure scope. This mattered-the fix required two engineers, not the whole team.\n\nMinimize blast radius: I split the team. Two engineers patched the specific vulnerability; the remaining four continued the checkout redesign. I resisted the urge to \"while we're in there\" expand scope-that's where sprint disruptions compound.\n\nCommunicate with specificity: I messaged stakeholders within the hour: \"Security fix ships tomorrow. Checkout redesign ships Thursday-3 days late.\" No vague \"we're working on it.\"\n\nProtect focus: I blocked non-security interruptions for 48 hours. Context-switching costs are real-one disruption is recoverable, a cascade isn't.\n\nMeasure and learn: Security fix shipped in 30 hours. Checkout shipped 3 days late, within communicated expectations. Post-sprint retro surfaced a gap in our pre-sprint security checklist for payment flows-we added a mandatory review gate, reducing similar P0s by roughly 60% over the next two quarters.\n\nTrade-off I owned: Splitting the team slowed both tracks slightly. The alternative-pausing the redesign entirely-would've been 5+ days late. Parallel workstreams was the right call given severity and team capacity.",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "domain": "fintech",
    "tracks": [
      "Product Operations",
      "B2B / Enterprise PM",
      "Internal Tools PM"
    ]
