    "q": "How do you evaluate the success of your own work as a PM?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "I evaluate PM success across two dimensions-process quality and outcome quality-because outcomes are partly luck, but process is fully within my control.\n\nProcess (ongoing):\nBefore I treat any outcome as a win, I pressure-test the decisions that produced it. Did I do discovery before committing to a solution? Did I define success metrics *before* launch, not after? Did I surface trade-offs explicitly or let ambiguity linger? I validate this by reviewing my pre-launch docs against what actually shipped-if the \"why\" shifted without a conscious decision, that's a process failure regardless of the result.\n\nOutcomes (30/60/90 days post-launch):\nI track whether the feature moved its primary metric, whether usage patterns matched our hypothesis, and which assumptions broke. When I can, I advocate for a clean A/B test at launch specifically so I have unambiguous signal rather than directional noise.\n\nHonest calibration:\nAfter each major initiative, I run a short written retro: *What would a senior PM, reviewing my decisions with the same information I had, say about my approach?* This separates good process from good luck-a feature can succeed despite poor process and fail despite sound judgment. I want to know which situation I'm actually in.\n\nI also ask my tech lead and design partner one specific question post-launch: *\"What's one thing I could have done differently in how I ran this?\"* Specificity is what makes the feedback actionable rather than polite.",
