    "q": "Tell me about a time you had to make a product decision without enough data.",
    "a": "When facing data gaps, I default to a framework: assess reversibility, identify proxies, and make uncertainty visible to stakeholders.\n\nOur analytics pipeline failed 10 days before a critical onboarding flow launch-right when I needed activation funnel data to finalize design decisions.\n\nI explicitly mapped the asymmetric risk: delaying meant losing 3 weeks of learning and breaking stakeholder commitments; launching with a recoverable design flaw meant faster detection post-launch. Since we weren't changing pricing or removing features, the downside was correctable.\n\nTo validate before committing, I identified three proxy signals replacing the broken metric: in-app help widget opens (confusion indicator), support tickets mentioning onboarding, and completion rates for the one measurable step. I also pulled qualitative data from 8 user interviews conducted the prior sprint.\n\nI structured the launch as a phased A/B test-releasing to 20% of new users first, with daily reviews of proxy metrics for 5 days before full rollout. This gave us a circuit breaker without delaying the launch date entirely.\n\nI told stakeholders explicitly: \"We're launching without our standard activation data. This is a fast experiment, not a validated release. We'll make a go/no-go call on full rollout by day 5.\"\n\nSuccess metrics: help widget opens below 15% of sessions, support tickets under 2% of new users, and step-completion rates above our historical baseline.\n\nWhen the pipeline restored, activation data confirmed the proxies-the new flow outperformed the old one.\n\nThe lesson: uncertainty is manageable when you make reversibility explicit and instrument alternatives before you need them.",
