    "q": "How would you approach launching your existing product in a new international market?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Validate → Localize → Pilot → Scale\n\nBefore committing resources, I'd run a structured market assessment scoring candidates on four dimensions: addressable market size, competitive intensity (entrenched locals vs. fragmented), regulatory complexity (data residency, sector licensing), and cultural proximity to markets where we have proven PMF. This prevents intuition-driven expansion.\n\nData validation first. I'd conduct 20 user interviews in the target market before any localization spend, alongside a 2-week competitive teardown. Key questions: how are users solving this today, what's their price sensitivity, and which workflows differ from our existing users? If interviews surface fundamentally different use cases, that's a signal to pause-not accelerate.\n\nPilot design. Rather than a full launch, I'd run a 90-day beachhead pilot targeting the segment most similar to our highest-retention cohort domestically. Specifically, a geo-restricted beta with 500 users, comparing a localization-light version against a fully localized variant (A/B test on payment methods, language, and support hours).\n\nSuccess metrics: activation rate within 7 days (target: within 15% of domestic baseline), D30/D90 retention (flag if retention lags >20%), support ticket volume per user (proxy for localization gaps), and NPS delta vs. domestic cohort.\n\nTrade-offs I'd flag: beachhead focus delays revenue at scale but prevents burning GTM budget on a product that doesn't yet fit. Full localization upfront costs 3-4x more but reduces churn risk. I'd recommend the staged approach unless competitive pressure demands speed.",
