    "q": "How do you design for power users without alienating casual users?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Progressive Depth Architecture\n\nDesign for both by separating surface from depth-defaults serve casual users, advanced capabilities remain discoverable but non-intrusive.\n\nBefore designing anything, validate the actual split. Analyze behavioral data to understand what percentage of users are power vs. casual, their retention curves, and revenue contribution. At many B2B products, 20% of power users drive 60%+ of revenue-that changes prioritization significantly.\n\nConcrete approach:\n- Default layer: Clean, immediate value for day-1 users (Gmail's basic send/receive)\n- Behavioral triggers: Surface advanced features at usage milestones-after 20 completed workflows, surface keyboard shortcuts and bulk actions, not on day 3\n- Separate entry points when necessary: Distinct \"simple/advanced\" modes or consumer UI + API access when user types are genuinely incompatible\n\nPilot to validate: A/B test progressive disclosure triggers against static feature availability. Measure: power user feature adoption rate, casual user 30-day retention, and support ticket volume (complexity proxy). Run for 6 weeks across 10,000 users minimum.\n\nKey trade-off to acknowledge: Progressive disclosure risks burying features power users need immediately. If power users are your P1 revenue driver, invert the model-design for them first, ensure casual users extract enough value to eventually graduate upward.\n\nThe failure mode to avoid: \"compromise design\" that fully satisfies neither segment. Separation is better than averaging.",
