    "q": "How do you approach setting up the right product metrics infrastructure for an early-stage product?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Start with a measurement plan before touching any tooling-define the three decisions you need to make in the next 90 days, then work backward to the metrics that inform each decision. Everything else follows from that.\n\nInfrastructure setup in practice:\n\n1. Decision-first instrumentation. For each decision (e.g., \"Is activation working?\"), define the specific metric, then instrument only the events that answer it. Rich event properties from day one-user ID, timestamp, acquisition channel, session context-because retrofitting segmentation onto historical data is often impossible.\n\n2. Data validation before trusting anything. Instrument an event, then manually QA 20-30 sessions to confirm the numbers match reality. Teams that skip this discover six months later their core activation metric was double-counting.\n\n3. Pilot with three metrics, not thirty. Run a 30-day experiment: pick one north star and two supporting metrics, build a single shared dashboard, assign explicit ownership. Measure decision quality and alignment speed versus the prior ad-hoc period.\n\n4. Metrics to track success of the infrastructure itself: dashboard query frequency, percentage of team decisions citing data, time-to-answer for common product questions, and metric definition disputes per sprint.\n\nTrade-offs to acknowledge: Lightweight tools (Mixpanel + Stripe) ship faster but create migration pain at scale. Over-investing in warehouse infrastructure early burns engineering time when the product hypothesis isn't validated. The correct bet at early stage is high-quality event tracking with simple tooling-infrastructure can scale, missing historical data cannot.",
