    "q": "How would you design the product experience for a marketplace that needs to serve both sellers and buyers simultaneously?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Design two distinct products sharing one liquidity engine, then validate assumptions before scaling.\n\nI separate the experience into three layers: seller-side tools, buyer-side tools, and the shared trust/liquidity infrastructure connecting them.\n\nStart with data validation. Before building, I'd audit 20-30 user sessions per side to identify the highest-friction moments. Hypothesis: sellers churn from poor demand visibility; buyers churn from insufficient supply confidence. Analytics should confirm this before committing engineering resources.\n\nDistinct surfaces, unified data layer. Sellers are running businesses-they need demand signals (search volume, saves, request trends). Buyers are solving personal needs-they need curated selection and trust signals. Conflating these in one interface produces mediocrity for both.\n\nPilot: A/B test demand transparency for sellers. Show cohort A real-time buyer search data on their dashboard; withhold from cohort B. Measure 30-day seller listing activity and GMV contribution. Success threshold: 15% lift in active listings.\n\nKey metrics: Seller retention at day-30/90, buyer repeat purchase rate, transaction completion rate, off-platform escape rate, and trust score (review completion %).\n\nTrade-offs I'd flag: More seller data visibility accelerates supply quality but risks sellers gaming the algorithm. Payment protection builds buyer trust but increases operational cost. The \"escape the marketplace\" problem requires making on-platform value-protection, reviews, dispute resolution-exceed the fee cost, not just matching it.\n\nIncentive alignment is structural: ranking, fees, and communication tools should reward behavior that benefits both sides simultaneously.",
