    "q": "How do you approach building a feedback loop between customer success and product?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Structured Capture → Validated Signal → Closed Loop\n\nStart by standardizing what CS captures. Unstructured Slack messages don't scale. I implement a lightweight intake form: account name, pain point, workflow impact, and category (bug/feature/process). This feeds directly into the product backlog.\n\nData validation is critical before acting. Raw CS feedback is anecdotal until quantified. I cross-reference themes against usage data, support ticket volume, and churn reasons. If five enterprise accounts cite the same friction but product analytics show low engagement with that feature, that's a prioritization signal worth investigating further.\n\nPilot: Run a 90-day A/B test on closing the loop explicitly-when shipped work addresses a CS-sourced pain point, CS notifies the customer directly. Measure NPS delta, expansion revenue, and CS-submitted feedback volume against a control group. My hypothesis: visible impact motivates CS to submit higher-quality feedback.\n\nSuccess metrics: feedback-to-roadmap conversion rate (target: 15%), time-from-capture-to-triage (target: under two weeks), customer acknowledgment rate, and CS rep submission consistency.\n\nTrade-off to acknowledge: Weighting feedback by account tier risks systematically ignoring signals from smaller accounts that predict broader market shifts. I balance this by flagging high-frequency low-tier patterns separately.\n\nI also join 2-3 customer calls monthly to ask discovery questions CS wouldn't naturally ask-no feedback summary replaces direct exposure.",
