    "q": "How do you evaluate whether a product should expand into an adjacent market or deepen the current one?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "This is a capital allocation decision. I use a three-part framework: market saturation, adjacency leverage, and reversibility cost.\n\nStep 1: Measure current market ceiling first.\nIf we've captured less than 20% of our realistic serviceable market and retention is strong, deepening almost always wins-there's more accessible growth than we can exhaust. If penetration is high and growth is decelerating (revenue growth <10% QoQ in core segment), adjacency becomes urgent.\n\nStep 2: Validate the adjacency hypothesis before committing resources.\nRun a 4-week discovery sprint: 20+ interviews with adjacent segment users, a landing page test measuring conversion intent, and pricing sensitivity research. Key questions: Do they have the problem we think they have? Does our existing product provide 60%+ of the solution without major changes?\n\nStep 3: Pilot with a constrained A/B structure.\nAllocate one small team (2 engineers, 1 designer) for one quarter. Success metrics: adjacent segment activation rate >30%, NPS >40, CAC payback <18 months, and critically-zero degradation in core segment retention.\n\nThe real trade-off: Adjacent expansion consumes roadmap capacity and organizational attention. Figma and Stripe both went deep in their core segment first, which built the distribution and credibility that made adjacency defensible later.\n\nIf current penetration is below 20% with strong retention signals, deepen. Otherwise, validate and pilot.",
