    "q": "How do you bring alignment across engineering, design, and business when they have conflicting priorities?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Shared outcome first, then structured bets, then sequenced execution.\n\nWhen engineering, design, and business conflict on onboarding redesign priorities-tech debt vs. new visual system vs. a 4-week campaign deadline-I anchor everyone on one measurable goal before any solution discussion: trial-to-paid conversion rate.\n\nI run a 90-minute structured session where each function frames their proposal as a testable bet: \"We believe [approach] drives [outcome] because [evidence].\" This shifts the conversation from advocacy to reasoning.\n\nBefore committing, I validate assumptions. I pull current funnel data to identify where conversion actually breaks. If auth errors cause 15% drop-off, engineering's debt argument becomes evidence-backed. If users abandon at step three due to UI confusion, design's case strengthens. Data arbitrates opinion.\n\nThen I propose a sequenced pilot: two weeks of targeted debt reduction on the specific auth paths users touch during onboarding, followed by a phased design rollout prioritizing the two highest drop-off screens, with the campaign deadline intact.\n\nSuccess metrics: trial-to-paid conversion (primary), auth error rate during campaign window, time-to-first-value, and post-launch iteration velocity.\n\nTrade-offs I name explicitly: the phased design approach means inconsistent UI temporarily-acceptable if conversion improves. Skipping full debt paydown carries residual incident risk-mitigated by scoping debt work narrowly.\n\nMost cross-functional conflict is a framing problem. When everyone reasons from shared evidence toward a shared metric, alignment follows.",
