    "q": "What does good product operations look like for a product team at scale?",
    "a": "Good product ops at scale requires four interlocking systems: decision rights, shared data infrastructure, knowledge management, and launch discipline. Without all four, teams fragment as headcount grows.\n\nDecision rights first. Document explicitly which decisions PMs own unilaterally, which require cross-functional alignment, and which need leadership. Before codifying anything, validate the current state: audit two months of Slack escalations to identify where decisions are actually stalling. If 60%+ involve roadmap trade-offs that should be PM-owned, the framework needs to push authority down.\n\nShared metrics layer. Not just dashboards-a canonical definitions document (\"DAU means X on surface A, Y on surface B\"). Run a 30-day pilot: compare time-to-answer for product questions before and after deploying a shared metrics dictionary. Target: reduce metric-related Slack questions by 40%.\n\nSearchable research repository. Assign ownership, build an intake process, prune quarterly. Measure: percentage of new feature specs that cite prior research (baseline vs. 90 days post-implementation).\n\nCalibrated launch checklist. Tier launches by risk. Every tier requires pre-defined success metrics and a rollback trigger. A/B test checklist adoption across two PM pods-measure post-launch incident rate and time-to-rollback.\n\nThe trade-off: Process creates consistency but risks slowing high-velocity teams. The mitigation is quarterly calibration-reviewing whether the framework is accelerating or blocking decisions, and adjusting accordingly. Good product ops is itself a product: it needs an owner, metrics, and iteration.",
