    "q": "How do you structure and run an annual product planning process across multiple teams?",
    "a": "Framework: Evidence-in, Bets-out, 8-week cycle\n\nAnnual planning fails when it's calendar-driven rather than evidence-driven. My process runs 8 weeks pre-fiscal year across four stages.\n\nWeeks 1–2: Data validation before strategy. I synthesize three inputs in parallel: user evidence (what discovery and analytics confirmed, refuted, or left unresolved), business constraints (headcount, platform commitments, growth targets from leadership), and team health audits (tech debt load, squad capacity). No strategy memo gets written until these are complete. Skipping this step produces plans that are aspirational fiction.\n\nWeeks 3–4: One-page strategy memos per area. Each PM lead writes their top three bets, the hypothesis behind each, and explicit kill criteria-what would force us to change the plan mid-year. I consolidate into a portfolio view surfacing conflicts, dependencies, and funding gaps.\n\nWeeks 5–6: Leadership alignment on trade-offs, not line items. The key decision: platform investment vs. feature velocity. I force this trade-off to be explicit and documented, not deferred.\n\nWeeks 7–8: Squad-level quarterly themes. Q1–Q2 are committed. Q3–Q4 are directional. For any major bet, I propose a Q1 A/B test or 6-week pilot to validate core assumptions before full-year resource commitment.\n\nSuccess metrics: strategy brief completion rate, roadmap confidence scores by quarter, and mid-year plan retention (target: 70%+ of Q3 themes unchanged).\n\nCore trade-off: rigor versus speed. Tighter evidence requirements slow planning but dramatically reduce mid-year pivots.",
