    "q": "How do you balance building features for today's users vs. building to attract the users you want in 3 years?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "This is a portfolio allocation problem. My framework: explicitly split the roadmap by strategic user cohort, validate assumptions before scaling investment, and treat the tension as a signal to optimize-not resolve.\n\nThe allocation approach:\nStart by classifying current users against your 3-year ICP. Users already aligned with your future target justify investment that serves both horizons simultaneously-reliability improvements, security certifications, API depth. These are high-efficiency bets. Users outside your future ICP still fund operations, so don't abandon them, but cap investment proportionally.\n\nData validation first:\nBefore allocating roadmap to \"future users,\" validate they actually exist at scale. Run discovery interviews with 15-20 target segment prospects. Measure: conversion rate from pilot outreach, willingness-to-pay signals, and feature gap overlap with current users. If >40% of needed capabilities already serve your current base, the greenfield investment is smaller than assumed.\n\nPilot structure:\nAllocate 15-20% of one quarter's roadmap explicitly to future-user capabilities. A/B test messaging and onboarding for that segment against your existing funnel. Track new-segment activation rate, time-to-value, and 30-day retention separately from core cohort metrics.\n\nSuccess metrics: new ICP segment activation, current-user churn delta during the experiment, and revenue contribution ratio between cohorts at 6 months.\n\nThe trade-off: Over-investing in future users starves current retention; under-investing means competitors own that segment before you arrive. The explicit allocation forces the honest conversation.",
