    "q": "How do you create and maintain a competitive intelligence system for your product area?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Signal-Before-Impact competitive intelligence, structured around three horizons.\n\nStart by scoping deliberately: 2-3 direct competitors (same problem, same segment), 1-2 indirect (same job-to-be-done, different solution), 1-2 analogs from adjacent markets. Anything broader creates noise, not insight.\n\nStructured inputs, not ad hoc scanning:\n- *Product changes:* Monthly review of release notes, G2/Capterra sentiment shifts, and hands-on trials\n- *Strategic signals:* Job posting analysis (hiring patterns reveal investment areas), pricing page change alerts, leadership content\n- *Customer voice:* Win/loss interviews, churn reasons from CS, review site trend shifts\n\nValidation before action: Before any competitive finding influences the roadmap, I require corroboration from at least two independent sources-one internal (sales/CS signal) and one external (market data). Single-source competitive claims are the most common way bad decisions enter planning.\n\nPilot: When launching this system on a new product area, I'd run a 90-day A/B test: one squad receives structured monthly competitive briefs embedded in planning docs; another continues ad hoc. Success metrics: roadmap decisions with documented competitive context (target: 70%+), win rate on contested deals, and time-to-awareness of material competitor changes (target: under 30 days).\n\nTrade-offs: This system costs roughly 10-15% of one PM's time. The risk is over-indexing on competitor moves versus customer problems. I counterbalance by requiring every competitive response to map to a validated customer need-otherwise it's mimicry, not strategy.",
