    "q": "How do you define the operating model for a product team  . who does what, and how do decisions get made?",
    "a": "An operating model is the decision-making and coordination infrastructure that lets a team execute without escalation debt. I define it across four explicit dimensions.\n\nDecision rights by reversibility and impact. Reversible, low-impact decisions: PM decides, informs async. Medium-impact: PM decides with tech lead and designer input within 24 hours. Hard-to-reverse or high-impact: PM recommends, CPO approves with documented rationale. Before finalizing this, I audit the last 10 escalations to identify where decision rights were actually unclear-this data often reveals the real gaps versus the perceived ones.\n\nMinimal meeting cadence. Daily async standup, weekly planning with written pre-read, bi-weekly retro, monthly stakeholder review. Every meeting has an owner, a decision output, and a 30-minute default. I track meeting load per person quarterly and cut anything producing no documented decisions.\n\nExplicit role boundaries. I own problem definition, success metrics, prioritization, and stakeholder communication. Engineering and design own solutions within defined constraints. When I have strong solution opinions, I frame them as constraints with reasoning, not directives.\n\nConflict resolution protocol. Competing views get stated explicitly, the separating assumption gets identified, and we design the fastest test-usually a two-day spike or user session-to resolve it.\n\nTrade-off I acknowledge: More explicit structure adds overhead upfront and can feel bureaucratic on small teams. The right call is to pilot this on one team for one quarter, measuring decision cycle time and escalation frequency before scaling. Success threshold: 30% reduction in escalations, decisions documented within 48 hours.",
