    "q": "How do you think about managing across two or more product areas simultaneously as a lead PM?",
    "a": "Managing multiple product areas requires a portfolio mindset, not a parallel PM mindset. The failure mode is optimizing each area independently while missing cross-area dependencies and resource conflicts.\n\nMy framework has three layers:\n\n1. Portfolio-level prioritization. I maintain a single cross-area priority stack ranked by urgency and foundational impact. Before doing this instinctively, I validate it with data-reviewing each area's key metrics (activation, retention, revenue contribution) to confirm my intuitions about where the highest leverage problems actually are.\n\n2. Dependency management. I run a 30-minute weekly cross-area sync with a live dependency map. When I introduced this at my last company, we caught a breaking API conflict between two teams three weeks before launch-previously that would have surfaced in QA.\n\n3. Distributed ownership with clear decision boundaries. I delegate meaningful authority to senior ICs and associate PMs, defining explicitly what they own versus what escalates to me. To validate this structure works, I pilot it in one area first-measuring decision velocity (time from identified problem to shipped solution) and escalation rate as proxies for whether the model is calibrated correctly.\n\nKey metrics I track portfolio-wide: cross-area dependency resolution time, per-area OKR health, and stakeholder surprise rate (how often leadership hears something in a review they didn't expect).\n\nThe core trade-off: depth versus breadth. Distributed ownership scales capacity but creates narrative fragmentation risk-which is why I present areas to leadership as a single coherent portfolio story, not separate updates.",
