    "q": "How would you build a product roadmap for a team that's launching in a new geographic market?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Discover → Validate → Expand\n\nBefore any roadmap work, I'd conduct 10–15 in-market user interviews to answer three questions: Does our core value proposition translate? What's broken or missing? What alternatives do users currently rely on? Without this, the roadmap is guesswork.\n\nFrom discovery, I'd build two parallel tracks:\n\nTrack 1: Localization (language, currency, compliance, payments) - table stakes, largely technical, non-negotiable before launch. Compliance requirements like GDPR have long lead times and block everything downstream if missed.\n\nTrack 2: Market-fit features - informed by discovery, prioritized by one metric: can this acquire and retain our first 100 committed users in the beachhead segment?\n\nValidation step: Before scaling Track 2, I'd run a geo-targeted A/B test - baseline product plus localization versus baseline plus localization plus one market-specific feature - measuring 30-day retention and activation rate as primary success metrics, with CAC as a guardrail.\n\nTrade-offs I'd make explicit with leadership: Optimizing for beachhead depth over broad feature coverage means slower revenue ramp but higher retention signal. Building compliance early delays feature work by 4–6 weeks but eliminates legal exposure that could shut down the launch entirely.\n\nSuccess looks like: 100 retained users at 40%+ 30-day retention before expanding the roadmap scope - not launch date or feature count.\n\nLocal feedback loops (advisory panel, native-language support channel) feed directly into the next roadmap iteration.",
