    "q": "Describe your approach to running a product team's quarterly planning process.",
    "a": "Framework: Four-phase quarterly planning built on validated inputs, not assumptions.\n\nWeek 1 - Input gathering: Synthesize three data streams simultaneously: business performance (OKR attainment, revenue delta, strategy shifts), user signal (90-day research synthesis, support ticket themes, NPS movement, CS call patterns), and team capacity (engineering availability after debt/infrastructure commitments, design bandwidth, PM time). Simultaneously audit competitive shifts that may reprioritize existing roadmap items.\n\nWeek 2 - Bet construction with explicit validation: I write a strategy memo proposing 3–5 bets, each containing: problem statement, solution hypothesis, expected impact tied to a specific metric, investment required, and the single key assumption that could invalidate it. Before broader review, I run a lightweight data validation step-confirming each bet is grounded in at least two independent signals, not stakeholder opinion alone.\n\nWeek 3 - Structured review with tradeoff documentation: Engineering and design pressure-test feasibility and surface scope risks. Stakeholder review focuses on defending priority tradeoffs explicitly: \"We chose X over Y because Y's impact is 3x lower against our Q-target.\" Decisions are documented, not implied.\n\nWeek 4 - Finalize, pilot, and communicate: Lock the roadmap and identify one bet appropriate for an A/B test in the first four weeks-establishing a measurement baseline early. Success metrics: roadmap stability (fewer than two major scope changes mid-quarter), team confidence score (post-planning survey ≥8/10), and OKR attainment rate at quarter-end.\n\nKey tradeoff: This process takes four weeks and requires PM discipline to protect. In fast-moving environments, compress to two weeks-but never skip the validation and tradeoff documentation steps.",
