    "q": "How do you manage product strategy when your company has both a freemium product and an enterprise product targeting the same users?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Unified core, separated surfaces - with deliberate tier governance.\n\nIn PLG + enterprise hybrids, the core failure mode is letting the two tracks drift into either feature cannibalization or UX incoherence. I manage this with four explicit decisions:\n\n1. Tier boundary ownership. I designate a single person responsible for the freemium/enterprise feature boundary. Without clear ownership, sales pressure erodes freemium value and undermines the bottom-up motion.\n\n2. Data validation first. Before any tier-boundary change, I analyze conversion funnel data: which freemium features correlate with enterprise upgrade events? At Slack/Figma-style companies, this is typically collaboration or admin features - not core utility. That data governs tier placement, not intuition.\n\n3. Pilot the boundary. I'd run an A/B test on one candidate feature - say, SSO or audit logs - gating it enterprise-only for one segment while exposing it broadly to another. Success metrics: enterprise conversion rate, freemium retention delta, and support ticket volume as a proxy for perceived value loss.\n\n4. Separate North Stars, shared portfolio view. Freemium tracks activation rate and 30-day engagement; enterprise tracks NRR and expansion ARR. I review both monthly against a single company growth target.\n\nTrade-off to name explicitly: Expanding freemium accelerates top-of-funnel but can lengthen enterprise sales cycles if buyers see less differentiation. That tension never fully resolves - it requires quarterly recalibration, not a one-time decision.",
