    "q": "How would you structure a product review process that's lightweight but still surfaces real risks?",
    "a": "Framework: Tier-Based Review with Structured Risk Surfacing\n\nStructure reviews around decision stakes, not process uniformity. Three tiers:\n\n- Tier 1 (pricing, market entry, major API changes): 1-2 page written brief, cross-functional input, executive sign-off, designated red-team reviewer\n- Tier 2 (new features, significant scope changes): PM-led async review with eng and design leads, documented outcome\n- Tier 3 (bug fixes, copy tweaks, A/B variants): PM decides, team informed via changelog\n\nData validation built in: Every Tier 1 and 2 brief requires explicit statement of assumptions and the data supporting them-not just \"users want this\" but the specific research, metric, or signal. This forces teams to distinguish validated beliefs from guesses before review, not during.\n\nPilot approach: Run this tiering system for one quarter on a single product area. Measure: review cycle time, decision reversal rate post-launch, and stakeholder escalation frequency. Compare against baseline from previous quarter.\n\nSuccess metrics: Decisions reversed after shipping drop below 10%; Tier 1 reviews complete within 5 business days; zero major incidents attributable to skipped review.\n\nKey trade-off: Red-team adversarial review adds 1-2 days to Tier 1 decisions. That friction is intentional-it's cheap compared to a mis-launched pricing change. The risk is teams gaming tier classification to avoid scrutiny, which quarterly decision retrospectives catch by auditing outcomes against tier assignments.",
