    "q": "How do you approach competitive intelligence as a PM practice, not just a one-time exercise?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Competitive intelligence should be a continuous, structured practice-not a quarterly ritual. I organize it around three layers: signals, synthesis, and action.\n\nSignals (automated, ongoing): I set up Google Alerts for competitor launches, RSS feeds for competitor blogs, G2/Capterra review monitoring, and LinkedIn job posting alerts to identify investment areas. This creates a real-time stream requiring maybe two hours per week to triage.\n\nSynthesis (monthly): I maintain a one-page competitive card per competitor-positioning, target segment, win/loss patterns, and feature gaps-updated monthly, not quarterly. Critically, I run a monthly sales-product roundtable to capture competitive objections from the field. Sales reps hear these conversations daily; they're more valuable than any feature teardown I can do myself.\n\nAction (quarterly): Competitive findings feed directly into roadmap prioritization, not just marketing talking points. When a competitor ships something that resonates, I treat it as market validation of an underlying user need, not a feature to copy.\n\nData validation: I cross-reference three sources before acting-customer interviews, win/loss data, and public reviews-to avoid over-indexing on a single competitor's move.\n\nPilot: I'd run a 60-day test comparing win rates in segments where we've distributed structured competitive cards to sales versus those without. Metrics: win rate delta, deal cycle length, and competitive objection frequency in CRM notes.\n\nTrade-off: This practice requires consistent maintenance discipline. If it slips, the cards go stale and trust erodes fast-so I assign ownership, not committees.",
