    "q": "How would you design the ideal PM-engineering relationship for a high-performance product team?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Build a shared problem-solving system, not a handoff process.\n\nBefore redesigning the relationship, I'd validate where friction actually lives. I'd survey engineers and PMs separately using a structured diagnostic-rating collaboration quality across discovery, scoping, and delivery-then cross-reference with output data: rework rates, scope changes mid-sprint, and feature adoption within 90 days. This surfaces whether the problem is upstream (discovery) or downstream (execution).\n\nBased on that data, I'd pilot two structural changes with one team over one quarter:\n\n1. Problem-first briefings. Replace solution specs with problem briefs: user evidence, success metric, and constraints only. Engineers own solution design. Measure: reduction in mid-sprint scope changes and increase in engineer-rated ownership scores.\n\n2. Engineers in discovery. Engineers observe two user research sessions per cycle-not conducting, observing. Measure: technical constraint flags raised before sprint planning versus after (targeting 80%+ flagged early).\n\nTrade-offs to acknowledge: Problem-first briefings slow initial alignment but reduce rework. Engineering involvement in discovery increases time investment upfront but compresses late-stage surprises. Both require psychological safety to work-if engineers feel punished for raising constraints, neither change lands.\n\nShared success metric: Feature impact rate-percentage of shipped features that move their target metric within 60 days. This replaces delivery velocity as the team's north star and forces genuine joint accountability rather than siloed execution against separate definitions of done.",
