    "q": "How do you think about balancing short-term wins with long-term platform investment?",
    "a": "Framework: Treat this as a portfolio allocation problem with explicit ROI horizons.\n\nI use a structured 70/30 default split-70% near-term feature work, 30% platform investment-but I treat that as a hypothesis to validate, not a fixed rule.\n\nFirst, I validate before committing resources. I audit the last two quarters: How much engineering time went to workarounds and incident response? What's actual feature velocity versus 18 months ago? At one company, this surfaced that 40% of sprint capacity was absorbed by architectural debt-which immediately reframed the conversation from \"should we invest in platform?\" to \"we already are, just invisibly.\"\n\nThen I structure platform work to generate early signal. Rather than a 6-month black box, I stage investments with interim deliverables tied to measurable outcomes. Phase 1 unlocks a specific blocked feature; Phase 2 reduces onboarding iteration time; Phase 3 enables a new capability entirely. Each phase is an A/B test of sorts-did we recover the velocity we projected?\n\nMetrics I track: feature cycle time, unplanned work percentage per sprint, incident-to-resolution time, and engineer-reported friction scores.\n\nThe real trade-off: Platform investment delays near-term revenue but compounds across every future product. Short-term bias feels safe but creates invisible tax. The goal is making that tax visible-translating \"we need better infrastructure\" into \"this is costing us X engineering days and Y% velocity-here's the payback curve if we address it now.\"",
