    "q": "How would you approach re-platforming a product without disrupting existing users?",
    "a": "Framework: Strangler Fig + Graduated Traffic Migration\n\nRe-platforming succeeds when \"nothing changes for users\" is treated as a measurable constraint, not an aspiration. Here's my approach:\n\n1. Define Acceptance Criteria First\nBefore any code moves, I establish explicit non-negotiables: response times within 5% of baseline, zero regression in core workflow error rates, 100% feature parity verified in staging. These gate every migration phase.\n\n2. Validate Current State\nInstrument the existing platform to capture baseline metrics-p95 latency, error rates by workflow, conversion rates on critical paths. Without this, you're flying blind during cutover.\n\n3. Strangler Fig + Phased A/B Migration\nRoute 1% of traffic to the new platform targeting lowest-risk user segments (new accounts, non-paying users). Measure for 2 weeks against baseline before expanding to 10%, 25%, 50%, 100%. Each gate requires statistical significance on core metrics before proceeding.\n\n4. Success Metrics\n- Error rate delta < 0.1% versus control\n- p95 latency within 5% of baseline\n- Support ticket volume unchanged\n- Core conversion rates within 2% of control\n\n5. Trade-off Awareness\nSlower migration extends dual-platform maintenance costs and engineering complexity. I'd explicitly align with leadership: accept 4-6 weeks of parallel operation overhead in exchange for near-zero user disruption risk.\n\n6. Rollback Requirement\nRollback must be tested in staging before any production traffic moves. If we can't reverse in under 30 minutes, we don't ship.",
