    "q": "How do you use jobs-to-be-done (JTBD) framework in practice, and where does it fall short?",
    "a": "JTBD is a discovery tool, not a decision tool-I use it deliberately and pair it with validation.\n\nIn practice, I apply it in three ways:\n\n1. Reframing feature requests. When a stakeholder requests \"a search bar in settings,\" I ask: \"What job can't users complete?\" This often reveals multiple viable solutions, preventing premature convergence on one implementation.\n\n2. Expanding competitive analysis. JTBD forces honest competitive mapping. For a productivity app, the real competition isn't Notion-it's email, memory, and doing nothing. This surfaces non-obvious threats early.\n\n3. Sharpening positioning. \"Advance your career\" outperforms \"online courses\" because it's job-aligned, emotionally resonant, and harder to commoditize feature-by-feature.\n\nValidation step: Before acting on a proposed job, I test it with 8–10 customer interviews plus behavioral data (support tickets, drop-off points, workarounds). If users can't articulate the job unprompted, I treat the hypothesis as unvalidated.\n\nPilot approach: For any major job-aligned pivot, I run a 4-week A/B test measuring job completion rate, activation-to-retention, and NPS delta-not just engagement.\n\nWhere it falls short:\n- Underweights emotional and social jobs (feeling in control, signaling status)\n- Poorly suited to mature products where usage has drifted from original intent\n- Provides zero prioritization guidance-you still need an impact/effort framework to choose which jobs to serve\n\nJTBD sharpens the question. It doesn't answer it.",
