    "q": "How do you ensure that a product you own stays coherent as the team scales and more PMs join?",
    "a": "Framework: Coherence requires shared principles, structural rituals, and active ownership-not just good intentions.\n\nBefore scaling the PM team, I validate whether incoherence is actually a risk by auditing the current product: inconsistent terminology, diverging navigation patterns, or duplicate solutions to the same problem are early signals. I also measure baseline coherence through user research-specifically, task completion rates across product areas and qualitative feedback flagging \"feels disconnected.\"\n\nWhat I implement:\n\n1. Product principles document - Captures design patterns, UX tradeoffs, and core terminology before new PMs join. This is the shared constitution, not a style guide.\n\n2. Cross-PM coherence ritual - Lightweight weekly review for decisions touching shared surfaces: navigation, data models, terminology. Not a gate; a forcing function for conversation.\n\n3. Designated coherence owner - Lead PM with explicit authority to flag conflicts before they ship, not post-launch.\n\n4. Shared infrastructure - Design system, component library, and analytics stack. Coherence emerges naturally when foundations are shared.\n\nPilot: When onboarding the second PM, I'd A/B test structured coherence check-ins (30 min/week) versus async documentation-only. Success metrics: cross-area terminology consistency score, support ticket volume citing \"confusing\" UX, and new-user task completion parity across product sections.\n\nTrade-off: More process slows individual PM velocity. I accept that cost because the alternative-rebuilding coherence retroactively-is 3-5x more expensive in engineering and user trust.\n\nQuarterly end-to-end audits catch accumulated drift before it compounds.",
