    "q": "How do you think about user segmentation as a strategic tool, not just an analytics tool?",
    "a": "Strategic segmentation means deciding who the product is *for*, not just explaining why metrics differ across groups. Here's my framework:\n\nStart with the ICP as a design constraint, not a marketing artifact. When I define \"Series B+ SaaS companies with a dedicated data team\" as my ICP, that resolves ambiguous prioritization decisions-complexity, integrations, pricing tiers all become clearer. Equally important: it explicitly defines who I'm *not* building for, which is where most early products fail. Mediocre coverage across many segments beats nobody's threshold.\n\nValidate with cohort data before committing. I cross-segment retention and LTV by acquisition channel and behavioral signals to distinguish genuine product-market fit from low-quality acquisition noise. If SMB churns at 40% while enterprise holds at 85%, that's a strategic signal, not just an analytics footnote.\n\nPilot via a segment-focused A/B test. I'd run a 60-day experiment where ICP users receive a tailored onboarding and feature surface, measuring 30-day retention, expansion revenue, and NPS delta versus the control. Population-level A/B results routinely mask 15-20% effects within the ICP that are the only effects that matter strategically.\n\nSuccess metrics: ICP segment retention rate, LTV:CAC ratio by segment, and feature adoption depth among ICP versus non-ICP users.\n\nThe trade-off: Narrowing focus risks short-term revenue from adjacent segments. The bet is that a dominant position in one segment compounds faster than a mediocre position across many-which the cohort data should confirm before full commitment.",
