    "q": "How do you manage product quality and reliability as a PM responsibility, not just an engineering one?",
    "a": "Quality and reliability are PM responsibilities because product decisions-scope, timelines, and complexity tradeoffs-directly cause most quality failures.\n\nMy framework: Quality as a Product KPI, not an engineering afterthought.\n\nDefine standards before building, not at QA. Every PRD includes explicit acceptance criteria: error rate thresholds (<0.5% per session), p95 latency targets, and test coverage minimums. These are non-negotiable in the definition of done. When timelines compress, I cut scope-not quality gates.\n\nTrack quality metrics alongside growth metrics. Weekly reviews include: error rate per user session, P0/P1 bug count per sprint, MTTR, and support ticket volume by category. A quality regression gets the same escalation urgency as a retention drop.\n\nValidate assumptions with data before shipping. Before launching, I confirm real-world conditions match our quality assumptions-load testing under actual traffic patterns, not synthetic estimates. I've caught scope-driven risk this way.\n\nPilot rigorously. I run staged rollouts (5% → 20% → 100%) with automated rollback triggers if error rates exceed thresholds. This catches production failures before they become trust-destroying incidents.\n\nThe tradeoff I'm explicit about: Protecting quality standards means occasionally shipping later or smaller. I make this tradeoff visible to stakeholders upfront rather than negotiating it under deadline pressure-that's where standards collapse.\n\nFinally, I run product retrospectives after incidents: which product decisions created this failure? Scope too large? Edge cases missed in the spec? This closes the loop and prevents repeated failure patterns.",
