    "q": "How do you approach building product strategy in a market undergoing rapid technological change?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Anchor on durable user problems, build optionality into bets, optimize for learning velocity.\n\nRapid tech change creates both genuine platform shifts and costly distractions. My job is to distinguish between them.\n\nStep 1: Ground strategy in stable user problems, not technology. Instead of \"how do we use AI?\", I ask \"what problems were previously unsolvable that new capabilities now address?\" This prevents strategy from drifting with hype cycles.\n\nStep 2: Segment investment across three horizons. Near-term (0-12 months): deploy proven capabilities with known implementation costs. Mid-term (12-36 months): fund discovery and architecture for maturing technologies. Long-term (36+ months): scenario-plan without over-committing resources.\n\nStep 3: Validate assumptions before scaling. Before committing roadmap resources, I run a 6-8 week pilot-typically a modular feature with a 10-15% user cohort-measuring task completion rate, retention delta, and support ticket deflection. These reveal whether the technology solves a real problem or just a perceived one.\n\nStep 4: Build reversible architecture. APIs over embedded integrations, abstraction layers over hardcoded technology choices. This preserves optionality when-not if-the underlying technology shifts.\n\nStep 5: Watch revealed behavior, not stated preferences. Early adopters' actual usage leads stated preference by 12-18 months. I instrument behavioral signals over survey responses.\n\nKey trade-off: Moving fast on emerging tech risks building on unstable foundations; moving slowly risks ceding category position. The mitigation is modular bets with clear kill criteria defined upfront.",
