    "q": "How would you evaluate and respond to a feature request that comes with a large deal attached to it?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Evaluate the feature independently of the deal, then make a clear recommendation.\n\nWhen a large deal arrives with a feature attached, the first instinct-build it-is usually wrong. My process:\n\n1. Decouple the feature from the deal pressure. Would we build this feature if no deal were attached? If not, that's a signal, not a blocker.\n\n2. Validate the underlying need. I speak directly with the prospect's champion to understand the workflow, not the requested solution. Simultaneously, I query our CRM and support tickets to see if existing customers have raised similar pain points. If three or more accounts reflect the same need, the feature has broader legitimacy.\n\n3. Quantify full cost. Engineering time is visible; maintenance burden, codebase complexity, and the precedent for future deal negotiations are not. I surface all of these to sales leadership before any commitment.\n\n4. Propose a scoped pilot. If the feature has merit, I suggest building a lightweight version behind a feature flag for this customer only, with a 60-day instrumentation period measuring adoption rate, support ticket reduction, and whether the feature drives renewal intent-not just deal close.\n\n5. Make a clear recommendation: Build broadly, commit to roadmap, propose an alternative, or decline with reasoning.\n\nTrade-off I acknowledge: Declining risks the deal and damages the sales relationship. Accepting builds debt and sets a negotiation precedent. The right answer depends on whether the feature serves the product or just the quarter.",
