    "q": "Describe how you think about the relationship between product design and product strategy.",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Design and strategy are co-creative, not sequential. My framework: strategy sets the constraint space, design generates user evidence that validates or challenges it, and both iterate together.\n\nIn practice, this means three things:\n\nDesign informs strategy through discovery. Designers running usability tests and behavioral synthesis are producing the highest-quality user signals in the building. Any strategy built without that input is operating on incomplete data. I validate strategic assumptions by requiring design research artifacts-job-to-be-done interviews, friction logs, drop-off heatmaps-before locking roadmap priorities.\n\nStrategy constrains design purposefully. Which segment, which use case, which competitor we're differentiating from-these constraints prevent design from producing beautiful experiences that don't serve strategic goals. I'd A/B test this directly: measure task completion and conversion for design work done with explicit strategic constraints versus open-ended briefs. My hypothesis is 15-20% better conversion in the constrained condition.\n\nDesign quality is a measurable strategic moat. I track it via SUS scores, support ticket volume, and competitive NPS deltas-not aesthetics. These feed back into strategy reviews, not just retrospectives.\n\nThe core trade-off: involving design early in strategy slows initial velocity but reduces costly late-stage pivots. The failure mode to avoid is using design to rescue bad product decisions-polished onboarding cannot manufacture retention. Design compounds when it's a co-creator, not a finishing layer.",
