    "q": "How would you handle a situation where your product's growth is being driven by a use case you didn't design for?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Start with validation, then decide: support it, ignore it, or actively redirect it.\n\nWhen unintended growth appears, resist the urge to immediately celebrate or immediately build. First, verify it's real signal-not noise.\n\nValidate before acting:\nPull usage data to confirm the behavior is statistically significant (>10% of active users, sustained over 60+ days, not just a spike). Run qualitative interviews with 8–10 of these users to understand the job-to-be-done and why your product fits \"well enough.\"\n\nThen evaluate fit against three criteria:\n- Strategic alignment with your target market\n- Monetization viability (does this segment pay, or does it consume infrastructure?)\n- Conflict risk with your core user base\n\nIf the signal is strong, run a structured pilot: Dedicate one sprint to removing the top three friction points these users hit. A/B test the modified flow against the existing experience. Measure: activation rate for this use case, retention at 30/60 days, NPS delta versus core users, and revenue per account.\n\nThe trade-off is real: Optimizing for an emergent use case risks roadmap dilution, brand confusion, and alienating your designed-for users. Ignoring it risks ceding a legitimate market to a competitor who will build for it intentionally.\n\nMy recommendation: Treat it like a product hypothesis. Time-box discovery to 4 weeks, make an explicit build/don't-build decision with leadership, and if you don't build-communicate that clearly to affected users before product changes break their workflows.",
