    "q": "How do you approach product discovery when users can't articulate what they need?",
    "a": "When users can't articulate needs, I shift from asking to observing, inferring, and testing. My framework: observe behavior → analyze workarounds → validate with prototypes.\n\nStep 1: Behavioral observation over stated preference.\nSession recordings and contextual shadowing reveal the truth. A user claiming \"search works fine\" may actually export every result to a spreadsheet-that gap *is* the product requirement.\n\nStep 2: Workaround analysis.\nI ask: \"What do you do before and after using our product to complete this task?\" Any external tool or manual step signals an unmet need. The workaround's structure tells you exactly what to build.\n\nStep 3: Critical incident interviews.\n\"Walk me through the last time this workflow frustrated you.\" Specific, recent, emotionally charged incidents surface latent needs that hypothetical questions never reach.\n\nData validation: Before building, I quantify signal. If 60%+ of session recordings show the same workaround behavior, that's sufficient confidence to move forward.\n\nPilot: I run a 4-week A/B test on a low-fidelity prototype with 200 users from the workaround cohort, measuring task completion rate, time-on-task, and unprompted return usage within 7 days.\n\nSuccess metrics: 15%+ improvement in task completion, 20%+ reduction in time-on-task, and qualitative confirmation the workaround disappears.\n\nTrade-off: Observational discovery is slower and costlier than surveys. I use it selectively-when retention data suggests a problem exists but user feedback doesn't explain it.",
