    "q": "How do you think about the relationship between platform investment and product velocity  . and how do you make the case for platform work?",
    "a": "Platform investment is chronically undervalued because costs are immediate and benefits are diffuse. My framework addresses this by making the case in business terms, not engineering terms.\n\nStep 1: Quantify the velocity tax. \"We have technical debt\" loses to \"every new partner integration costs 3 weeks; 8 partners in the pipeline equals 24 weeks of preventable work.\" Before making any case, I validate this with data-pulling actual cycle time logs, counting integration touchpoints, and pressure-testing the estimate with engineering leads.\n\nStep 2: Tie platform to specific product unlocks. \"Building the ML feature store unblocks three Q3 roadmap initiatives requiring real-time inference\" is a business case. \"Refactoring the data layer\" is not. Same work, different frame.\n\nStep 3: Model compounding returns. I build a simple range model: if this platform investment accelerates 10 future initiatives by even 10-15%, what's the cumulative time saved? Precision isn't required-a credible range is enough to act.\n\nStep 4: Pilot before full commitment. I propose a time-boxed A/B: one team gets the new platform capability, one team ships the same feature type without it. Metrics I track: cycle time per feature, defect rate, and integration touchpoints required.\n\nStep 5: Own the tradeoff publicly. \"We're allocating 25% of Q3 capacity to platform, which delays X feature by 6 weeks. Here's why that's the right bet.\" Leadership silence on platform work is the worst outcome-it becomes invisible until it fails.",
