    "q": "Describe your approach to product strategy in a space with rapidly shifting technology  . particularly AI.",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Rapidly shifting AI technology demands a strategy that's firm on user outcomes, flexible on technical implementation. Here's my framework:\n\n1. Separate problem layer from solution layer.\nUser problems are more stable than technologies. \"Analysts spend 70% of their time on data cleaning rather than insight generation\" holds true whether the solution is SQL, BI tooling, or LLMs. Strategy anchored in the problem survives technology shifts.\n\n2. Validate before committing.\nBefore doubling down on any AI capability, I run lightweight experiments to confirm the technology actually moves core metrics-task completion rate, time-on-task, error reduction-not just impressiveness in demos. AI features that wow in prototype often underperform in production workflows.\n\n3. Invest in proprietary moats, not commodity AI.\nThe moat is proprietary data, deep workflow integration, or domain trust-not the model itself. I'd pilot this by A/B testing an AI feature using our proprietary training data versus generic prompting, measuring retention delta and NPS at 30 days.\n\n4. Sequence bets across a confidence gradient.\nI'd allocate ~70% capacity to high-confidence bets with current capability, ~30% to probabilistic bets contingent on model improvements. Committing majority roadmap to speculative capabilities is a compounding risk.\n\n5. Preserve architectural optionality.\nAbstraction layers over vendor APIs cost modest engineering overhead but prevent costly lock-in when better models emerge-an asymmetric trade-off worth making.\n\nThe honest tension: optionality and speed trade against each other. Moving cautiously loses first-mover advantage; I'd accept that trade to avoid building on disappearing foundations.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "domain": "general",
