    "q": "How do you approach designing a product strategy for a platform or ecosystem, where your success depends on third-party developers or partners?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Platform strategy requires a distinct framework: you're designing incentive structures for participants you don't control, not just features for users you serve directly.\n\nMy three-condition framework:\n\n1. Durable core value proposition. Developers make long-term bets. I validate this by interviewing 10-15 developers before launch to confirm our \"unfair advantage\" claim-what problem we solve for their users that they genuinely can't replicate. If fewer than 70% articulate the same value back, the proposition isn't clear enough.\n\n2. Calibrated openness. Too open degrades quality; too closed kills creative mass. I use a tiered model: core UX is tightly controlled, peripheral surfaces are open. The boundary placement is the single most consequential platform decision. I'd A/B test two governance tiers with a cohort of 50 developers, measuring 90-day retention and apps-per-developer as proxies for ecosystem health.\n\n3. Aligned value chain economics. I map explicitly where value flows and where it concentrates. Key metrics: developer revenue per monthly active user, partner churn rate, and NPS segmented by developer tier.\n\nTrade-offs I watch actively:\n- Revenue capture vs. developer trust (platform tax creep kills ecosystems slowly, then suddenly)\n- Stability commitments vs. platform evolution speed\n- Competing with developers on adjacent features vs. foregoing short-term revenue\n\nThe operating test: when developers succeed, does the platform's core metric improve proportionally? If that structural alignment breaks, no partnership program recovers it.",
