    "q": "What does organizational design look like for a product team, and how does it evolve as the company scales?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Product org design should follow product strategy-not historical accident. Structure teams around how you create value, then validate that structure with data before scaling it.\n\nCore Framework: Outcome Alignment > Output Alignment\n\nA team owning \"the payments page\" optimizes the payments page. A team owning \"user trust in financial transactions\" looks across pre-checkout confidence, processing reliability, and post-purchase transparency-a fundamentally larger leverage surface.\n\nValidation step: Before restructuring, audit where coordination costs actually live. Map cross-team dependencies over 90 days. If >30% of shipping blockers require 3+ team conversations, your structure is misaligned with your user journey.\n\nEvolution by stage:\n- 1–3 PMs: No subteams. Optimize for discovery speed, not coordination efficiency\n- 4–10 PMs: Split by user journey stage or segment. Each area needs a durable roadmap but must be movable by one PM\n- 10+ PMs: Add platform/enablement layer with formalized interfaces-shared API contracts, explicit SLAs between platform and product teams\n\nPilot approach: When restructuring, run a 60-day team-topology experiment on one product area before rolling out org-wide. Measure: shipping cycle time, cross-team dependency incidents, and PM-reported autonomy score.\n\nKey trade-off: Outcome-oriented teams increase PM scope and require stronger judgment-right structure with wrong talent creates chaos, not leverage.\n\nPeriodically ask: knowing our current strategy, would we design this structure from scratch today? If not, fix it.",
