    "q": "How do you identify and strengthen the core growth loop in a product?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Map → Measure → Optimize one loop before adding another.\n\nStart by identifying which loop actually drives your growth today-not the one you wish drove it. I audit three candidates: viral (user invites user), content (user-generated content attracts organic traffic), and integration (product surfaces in adjacent tools). Most teams discover their assumed loop isn't the real one.\n\nValidate with data first. Pull cohort data to find where compounding already exists-even weakly. If 10% of signups trace back to existing users sharing outputs, you have a viral loop worth strengthening. If SEO drives 40% of trials, content is your engine. Don't optimize a loop you haven't confirmed exists.\n\nPilot design. Run an A/B test on the highest-friction handoff point. For a viral loop, test a contextual share prompt triggered at the value-delivery moment versus a generic post-onboarding prompt. Primary metric: invite send rate. Secondary metrics: invite conversion rate, time-to-activation for invited users, and downstream 30-day retention of loop-sourced users.\n\nSuccess looks like: ≥15% improvement in yield (value events → acquisition inputs) without reducing core retention.\n\nTrade-offs to acknowledge. Aggressive sharing prompts increase invite send rate but can damage product experience and retention if mistimed-I've seen NPS drop 8 points from over-prompting. Optimize loop efficiency before loop volume. A tighter loop compounds; a leaky one just adds noise.",
