    "q": "How do you build an executive-level product narrative that connects product decisions to business outcomes?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Market Truth → Evidence → Bets → Risks → Decision\n\nExecutive narratives fail in two directions: feature lists that obscure strategy, or inspiring vision disconnected from decisions. The fix is a five-part structure that's specific enough to be credible and accessible enough to build confidence.\n\n1. Lead with market truth. \"Enterprises are replacing [category] based on [criterion] we're uniquely positioned to win\" beats \"we shipped 14 features.\"\n\n2. Connect strategy to observed reality. Not \"DAU +18%\" but \"DAU +18% post-onboarding redesign, with D30 retention 22 points higher than prior cohort-confirming we solved the activation problem diagnosed in Q3.\" Before finalizing any narrative, I validate these numbers directly with data and Finance to prevent credibility-destroying corrections mid-presentation.\n\n3. Make bets explicit. Name two or three major bets with resources committed, target metric and timeframe, and the evidence basis. This creates accountability and eliminates surprise.\n\n4. Name the risks honestly. Include one or two conditions that would trigger a strategy change and what signals we're watching. Executives receiving curated green-only decks disengage permanently.\n\n5. Close with the specific decision needed. \"I need your view on [X] to finalize Q3 allocation\" versus a passive awareness update.\n\nTrade-off to acknowledge: This level of transparency on bets and risks requires psychological safety with leadership. In low-trust environments, I'd pilot this approach with one sympathetic executive sponsor first, measuring whether decision velocity improves and whether follow-up questions shift from clarifying basics to genuine strategic input-the real signal that the narrative is landing.",
