    "q": "How do you manage the tension between customer-specific requests and building features that scale across your user base?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "My framework: every customer request gets classified before it gets prioritized-market signal, platform opportunity, or custom services engagement. That classification drives everything else.\n\nStep 1: Validate before assuming generalizability. When a request comes in, I talk to 8-10 similar customers within two weeks: \"Would this solve a real problem for you?\" If fewer than 30% independently confirm the underlying need, it's not a product problem-it's a services conversation. This prevents both over-building and dismissing legitimate signals.\n\nStep 2: Abstract the need, not the request. \"Custom export format\" is usually \"compliance system compatibility\"-which likely has a scalable integration solution. I always ask: what outcome are you actually trying to achieve? This opens solution space and often reveals a platform capability serving 20+ accounts instead of a one-off serving one.\n\nStep 3: Apply the configurability test. Can this be built as a configurable option another customer would use within 12 months? If yes, we design it as platform with this customer as pilot user and measure adoption by account two within 6 months. If no, it becomes a customer-funded custom development conversation.\n\nTrade-off I make explicit with sales: Building a custom feature typically costs 3-5 weeks of capacity. I quantify what that displaces-usually a capability projected to impact 25+ accounts-and force an explicit decision with full information.\n\nSuccess metrics: percentage of customer-requested features adopted by 3+ accounts within 12 months, and professional services revenue covering custom work costs.",
