    "q": "How do you conduct a product portfolio review to make investment decisions across multiple product lines?",
    "a": "A portfolio review should function as a structured capital allocation process, not a political negotiation. My framework: assess, validate, prioritize, decide, document.\n\nStructure:\n\nPre-work: Each product owner submits a standardized one-pager covering current metrics vs. targets, top three investment proposals with resource requirements and expected outcomes, and an honest assessment of what isn't working. Standardization is mandatory-it enables cross-portfolio comparison.\n\nSession 1 – Data Validation: Before any advocacy, we audit evidence quality. Are growth assumptions based on controlled experiments or hopeful extrapolation? I explicitly score each proposal on evidence strength (1-5). This exposes where we're making bets versus where we have conviction.\n\nSession 2 – Strategic Prioritization: I use a 2x2 matrix-strategic importance vs. evidence quality-to surface candidates worth funding. Key debate questions: Which investments create cross-portfolio leverage? Where is competitive timing critical?\n\nSession 3 – Allocation with Trade-offs Explicit: \"Funding X means not funding Y.\" Every decision stated in opportunity-cost terms.\n\nPilot Approach: For borderline investments, I propose a 6-week time-boxed pilot before full allocation, with predefined success metrics-activation rate, retention delta, revenue contribution-and a kill threshold.\n\nSuccess Metrics: Portfolio NPS, percentage of funded initiatives hitting 90-day milestones, and forecast accuracy quarter-over-quarter.\n\nTrade-off awareness: This process takes 3-4 weeks of leadership time. In high-velocity environments, I compress to async pre-work plus a single 3-hour session, accepting some loss of shared context for speed.",
