    "q": "How do you build a product thesis for a nascent market where user needs aren't well-defined yet?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Building a product thesis in a nascent market is a signal-detection problem. Here's my framework:\n\n1. Start with analog markets. Study spaces 5 years ahead of yours. Early Dropbox didn't invent file-sharing behavior-it studied FTP workarounds. Identify which segments adopted first and why. This generates hypotheses, not answers.\n\n2. Find workaround users immediately. Users hacking solutions with spreadsheets or manual processes reveal two things: problem acuity and your minimum viable bar. Interview 15-20 of these users before writing a single spec. If you can't find them, the market may not be ready.\n\n3. Map and rank critical assumptions. List the top 5 thesis assumptions-user behavior, willingness to pay, use frequency, distribution-then plot them on a 2x2: criticality vs. uncertainty. Ruthlessly prioritize the top-right quadrant. These are thesis-killers if wrong.\n\n4. Run a targeted pilot, not a broad launch. Pick the segment with the most acute pain and fastest feedback loop. Offer a concierge MVP to 50 users. Measure weekly active usage, retention at day-30, and qualitative effort-to-value ratio. This validates demand before you over-invest in infrastructure.\n\n5. Distinguish early adopter needs from mainstream needs explicitly. Building for early adopters' high pain tolerance all the way through is how you miss the chasm.\n\nThe core trade-off: Specificity accelerates learning but risks optimizing for an unrepresentative segment. Counteract this by documenting which assumptions are segment-specific versus market-wide from day one.",
