    "q": "How do you think about platform extensibility as a product design principle?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Platform extensibility is a strategic bet, not a feature-and most teams get it wrong by building APIs before validating developer demand.\n\nMy framework: Extensibility as a tiered commitment\n\nStart by validating whether developers actually want to build on your platform. Before any API investment, I'd instrument existing workarounds-are users exporting data to spreadsheets, using Zapier hacks, or requesting webhooks? That signal tells you where extensibility creates real value versus where it's aspirational.\n\nPilot approach: Launch a private beta with 10-15 high-intent developers on a single, low-risk API surface. Measure integration completion rate, time-to-first-call, and whether those integrations drive measurable retention lift in end users within 60 days.\n\nKey success metrics:\n- Developer activation rate (first successful API call within 7 days)\n- Integration-attributed user retention delta\n- Ecosystem NPS from third-party developers\n- API deprecation incidents per quarter (tracks technical debt accumulation)\n\nThe core trade-off: Every API surface is an implicit maintenance contract. More extensibility creates ecosystem lock-in and compounding network effects (Shopify's app store drives merchant acquisition), but constrains your ability to refactor core architecture. Salesforce's stability commitment became both its moat and its innovation ceiling.\n\nGovernance is the overlooked discipline: Build your deprecation policy, security review process, and marketplace quality standards *before* launch-not after ecosystem maturity forces your hand. Extensibility without governance creates platform liability, not platform value.\n\nDesign for the layer you are. If developers build on you, extensibility is the product.",
