    "q": "How do you approach defining the right level of product abstraction for a developer-facing product?",
    "a": "Framework: Match abstraction level to use case frequency, then validate with behavioral data before committing.\n\nStart with use case distribution analysis. Interview 15-20 developers across segments, then instrument your existing product or docs to quantify frequency. You'll typically find 70-80% of use cases cluster around 3-5 high-frequency patterns. Your abstraction should make those trivially easy-under 5 steps, zero documentation required.\n\nValidate the hypothesis before building. Recruit developers matching your target segments and run usability tests on competing abstractions. Measure time-to-first-successful-call and error rate. If senior developers abandon the high-abstraction path within 10 minutes, it's too limiting. If juniors stall on configuration decisions, it's too low.\n\nPilot with a progressive disclosure A/B test. Ship a high-abstraction default with an \"Advanced\" escape hatch surfacing raw configuration parameters. Measure: activation rate (first successful API call), 7-day retention, support ticket volume, and escape hatch usage. If >30% of users trigger the escape hatch within the first session, your default abstraction is too restrictive.\n\nTrade-offs to manage explicitly:\n- Higher abstraction accelerates onboarding but risks alienating power users who feel constrained\n- Lower abstraction maximizes flexibility but increases time-to-value and support burden\n- Progressive disclosure solves both but adds maintenance complexity-every new feature needs two surfaces\n\nWatch for abstraction drift over time. Quarterly, have a developer new to the product attempt core tasks unassisted. Sticking points are abstraction failures accumulating silently.",
