    "q": "How do you evaluate whether to invest in building a community around your product?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Evaluate community investment across three gates before committing resources.\n\nGate 1 – Strategic fit. Community creates compounding value when users benefit from shared knowledge (developer tools, creative platforms, professional skill-building) or peer accountability. It destroys value in transactional products (payments, ride-sharing) where users have no meaningful relationship to each other.\n\nGate 2 – Data validation before building. Before investing, I'd measure: organic community formation (are users already creating subreddits, Slack groups, Discord servers?), support ticket themes (are >30% peer-answerable questions?), and NPS comment sentiment for social language. Existing organic behavior is the strongest signal community will sustain itself.\n\nGate 3 – Pilot with a controlled A/B test. Seed a private community with your top 200 power users for 60 days. Measure retention delta versus control group, support deflection rate, and feature adoption from peer recommendations. This surfaces density problems cheaply before full investment.\n\nSuccess metrics: 30-day return posting rate (target >40%), questions answered within 24 hours (target >80%), and measurable retention lift in community members versus non-members.\n\nKey trade-offs: Community compounds over time but requires 12-18 months before ROI is visible-meaning it competes directly with near-term growth investments. It also creates obligations: a neglected community damages brand more than no community. If the pilot shows <20% engagement from seeded users, redirect budget to product-led growth instead.",
