    "q": "How do you think about building a product that has strong seasonal or cyclical usage patterns?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Seasonal products require a fundamentally different operating model than evergreen ones: optimize peak experience ruthlessly, engineer off-season retention deliberately, and measure year-over-year cohorts rather than month-over-month absolutes.\n\nMy framework has three priorities:\n\n1. Peak experience is the relationship-defining moment. Mediocre peak experiences don't generate returns next cycle. I allocate disproportionate design and engineering investment here-capacity planning, load testing, and documented degradation protocols become annual operational rituals, not afterthoughts.\n\n2. Off-season engagement must be intentional. Without deliberate design, off-season DAU collapses and re-engagement costs spike. I build lightweight habit-maintenance features-pre-season preparation tools, progress tracking, anticipation content-that keep the loop alive without demanding peak-level effort from users.\n\n3. Re-engagement timing must be calibrated. Too early generates noise and trains users to ignore the channel. Too late and competitors have already won them back. I map the user's decision timeline: what triggers seasonal intent, and how do I intercept that moment?\n\nValidation approach: Before committing to off-season features, I'd survey lapsed users to understand actual re-engagement triggers, then run an A/B test comparing early versus late re-engagement prompts against a control-measuring reactivation rate, first-session depth, and next-peak retention.\n\nKey trade-off: Off-season investment competes directly with peak-readiness investment. I'd use year-over-year cohort retention at equivalent seasonal moments-not raw DAU-to justify where to allocate.",
