    "q": "How would you define the PM career ladder and promotion criteria for a growing PM organization?",
    "a": "Framework: Define levels by observable behaviors × decision scope × influence radius-then validate before scaling.\n\nBefore finalizing any ladder, audit your current PM population. Map 8–10 respected PMs to draft level descriptions and check for clustering or gaps. If 60% land at \"Senior,\" your mid-levels are likely under-defined. This calibration data shapes the ladder before it shapes careers.\n\nFour-level structure (IC track):\n- PM: Executes within defined problem space; influences immediate team\n- Senior PM: Owns problem definition; influences cross-functional pod without authority\n- Staff PM: Improves decision quality across multiple teams; builds frameworks others adopt\n- Principal PM: Shifts organizational strategy; impact outlasts direct involvement\n\nEach level requires observable behavior anchors-not \"drives alignment\" but \"facilitates prioritization decisions accepted by three or more functions without escalation.\"\n\nPilot before institutionalizing: Run one promotion cycle with the new ladder on a single team. Measure inter-rater reliability: do two managers independently assessing the same PM land within one level? Target 80%+ agreement. Low agreement signals criteria are too abstract.\n\nSuccess metrics: Promotion rate consistency across demographics, 90-day retention post-promotion, manager confidence scores on calibration (survey), and reduction in promotion-appeal disputes.\n\nKey trade-off: Behavioral specificity increases fairness but risks rewarding documented performance over actual impact. Balance with outcome evidence requirements at each level-behaviors plus results, not behaviors alone.",
