    "q": "How would you design a product that serves users with significantly different levels of digital literacy?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Layered architecture with progressive disclosure, validated by literacy-stratified user research.\n\nBefore designing, I'd validate assumptions. Recruit participants across the literacy spectrum-specifically including occasional software users-and run task-completion sessions to identify where each cohort fails. This surfaces real friction points rather than assumed ones, and prevents designing for an imaginary average user.\n\nCore design principles:\n\nDefault to simplicity. Primary UI exposes only the 3-5 actions covering 80% of use cases. No jargon, sensible defaults requiring zero configuration, error messages that explain what happened and what to do next-not error codes.\n\nProgressive disclosure for complexity. Advanced settings, power-user modes, and configuration options exist but aren't prominent. Less-literate users never encounter complexity they didn't choose.\n\nContextual help explains purpose, not procedure. \"Saves your work automatically so you never lose progress\" outperforms \"Click save.\" Lower-literacy users understand buttons; they struggle with *why*.\n\nPilot: A/B test two onboarding flows-one with progressive disclosure, one exposing full functionality upfront. Segment results by literacy proxy (e.g., time-on-task during signup, device type). \n\nSuccess metrics: Task completion rate by literacy segment, support ticket volume, time-to-first-value, 7-day retention.\n\nTrade-off: Progressive disclosure risks frustrating power users who can't find advanced features. Mitigate with a visible \"Advanced\" entry point and search functionality, preserving depth without burying it.",
