    "q": "How do you approach the 'platform vs. product' decision  . when should a feature be a standalone product vs. part of the platform?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Three-Question Filter First\n\nBefore defaulting to platform integration (the path of least resistance), run three diagnostic questions:\n\n1. Distinct user? Does this feature serve someone with fundamentally different jobs-to-be-done than your core user?\n2. Distinct monetization? Does its value model conflict with the platform's pricing architecture (e.g., transaction-based inside a subscription)?\n3. Constrained growth? Would the platform's brand or positioning create an adoption ceiling?\n\nTwo or more \"yes\" answers signal standalone. Otherwise, integrate.\n\nValidate before deciding. Interview 20+ target users to confirm whether they perceive this as a separate tool or a platform enhancement. Check willingness-to-pay signals-separate pricing interest is a strong standalone indicator.\n\nPilot structure. Run a 60-day A/B test: ship the feature as a platform module to Cohort A, as a standalone beta to Cohort B. Measure activation rate, 30-day retention, expansion revenue, and support ticket volume per user.\n\nKey success metrics: feature DAU/MAU ratio, time-to-first-value, NPS delta versus baseline, and CAC if standalone requires separate GTM spend.\n\nTrade-offs to acknowledge explicitly: Standalone creates distribution costs and brand fragmentation risk. Integration risks constraining genuinely high-potential opportunities-Slack's standalone bet versus Microsoft Teams' integration strategy illustrates both outcomes. The decision should be driven by user architecture and monetization logic, never by implementation convenience.",
