    "id": "stpm-beh-21",
    "q": "Tell me about a time you influenced a company-level technical architecture decision as a product person.",
    "a": "My approach to influencing architecture decisions is to contribute business context that engineering cannot generate internally, then let the data change the conversation.\n\nThe situation: Engineering was evaluating whether to build a proprietary data storage layer for AI model training versus a cloud-managed alternative, scoring candidates on latency and cost at current scale. The proprietary layer was winning.\n\nMy intervention: I had roadmap visibility showing committed enterprise contracts implying 10x data volume growth within 18 months. Engineering's own estimates flagged the proprietary layer as a scaling bottleneck at that threshold-but that scenario wasn't in the evaluation matrix. Rather than arguing against the recommendation, I asked one question in the architecture review: does this analysis include our committed growth trajectory? It didn't.\n\nI proposed a two-week reanalysis using actual contract commitments as growth inputs-effectively an A/B comparison of both architectures at current versus projected scale.\n\nThe outcome: The updated model showed the cloud-managed alternative was more cost-effective at projected volume and eliminated a forced migration at the 18-month mark. The decision reversed.\n\nThe trade-off I acknowledged: cloud-managed introduced vendor dependency and slightly higher baseline latency-real costs I didn't minimize. But avoiding a mid-growth migration reduced execution risk on three flagship enterprise launches.\n\nKey metrics I tracked post-decision: storage cost per training run, migration incidents avoided, and time-to-scale for new enterprise onboarding.\n\nThe principle: product influence in architecture reviews means surfacing growth assumptions, customer commitments, and roadmap dependencies-not overriding technical judgment, but ensuring it has the right inputs.",
