    "id": "stpm-beh-22",
    "q": "How do you maintain strategic clarity for your product area when company priorities shift significantly each quarter?",
    "a": "When company priorities shift quarterly, I use a three-layer stability framework: an immutable anchor, a flexible roadmap, and a protected execution horizon.\n\nThe anchor is a 2-3 sentence strategic thesis-why this product area exists, what differentiated problem it solves, and what winning looks like in 24 months. I write it, socialize it with my director, and treat it as the filter for every incoming priority.\n\nData validation first: Before accepting a new priority, I ask: what signal triggered this shift? If it's one customer conversation or a single competitor move, I push for supporting data before resequencing. Undifferentiated urgency is the enemy of durable strategy.\n\nWhen conflict arrives: I escalate with explicit trade-off framing-\"Pursuing X means deprioritizing Y, which delays Z outcome by one quarter. Here's my recommendation.\" I never silently absorb competing priorities, because the downstream cost is team hedging and declining output quality.\n\nPilot proposal: For any major strategic addition, I negotiate a 6-week time-boxed spike before full roadmap commitment. We define success metrics upfront-typically activation rate, retention delta, or revenue signal-and make a go/no-go decision based on data, not momentum.\n\nMetrics I track: roadmap stability ratio (percentage of sprint goals unchanged week-over-week), team confidence scores in quarterly surveys, and delivery predictability.\n\nThe trade-off: This approach requires political capital. Pushing back on leadership priorities has a cost. I spend that capital selectively-only when the anchor is genuinely threatened, not for every uncomfortable shift.",
