    "id": "stpm-beh-23",
    "q": "Describe how you have used data to overturn a widely-held assumption about your product that leadership had held for years.",
    "a": "The assumption that enterprise churn was pricing-driven had persisted for three years, driving repeated discounting that eroded margin without solving the real problem.\n\nChallenging the narrative with data: Customer calls suggested a different story-churned accounts described workflow friction, not price. I validated this hypothesis systematically before escalating. I designed a 10-minute structured churn survey across 40 churned accounts and cross-referenced support ticket history for churned versus retained accounts in the 6 months pre-renewal.\n\nWhat the data showed: 67% of churned accounts had elevated support tickets in workflow categories tied to known product gaps. Price appeared in only 23% of responses-and primarily as a secondary factor. Critically, price-sensitive churners clustered outside our target segment; within it, workflow quality was the dominant driver.\n\nHonest about limitations: I presented findings with explicit caveats-survey self-reporting introduces rationalization bias, and correlation doesn't confirm causality. Leadership needed to weigh this appropriately.\n\nThe pilot: We ran a 6-month A/B-style experiment: engineering investment targeting the top 3 workflow friction points, measured against a control group receiving standard renewal support. Success metrics were renewal rate, time-to-resolution on workflow tickets, and NPS at renewal.\n\nOutcome: Renewal rates in the target segment improved 11 percentage points. Pricing concessions were reduced, recovering margin.\n\nThe trade-off: Redirecting engineering capacity carried opportunity cost against new feature development. But the retention ROI justified prioritization-retaining an enterprise account was worth 4–6x a new logo in that segment.",
