    "id": "dir-beh-10",
    "q": "Tell me about a time you made a hiring decision that didn't work out, and what you changed.",
    "a": "Hiring failures are process failures. Here's what I learned.\n\nI hired a Senior PM who interviewed exceptionally well-sharp product instincts, strong communication, credible track record. Six months in, she was struggling: couldn't operate in ambiguity, needed constant direction, and was creating real friction with engineering.\n\nWhere I failed: I ran a post-mortem and diagnosed two process gaps. First, every interview case I'd designed was too structured-I was testing product thinking in controlled conditions, not how candidates behave when the problem itself is undefined. Second, I'd relied entirely on candidate-supplied references, which are inherently biased toward positive signal.\n\nWhat I changed: I added one unstructured ambiguity exercise-no defined problem, no guardrails-and committed to at least one back-channel reference per senior hire. To validate the fix, I tracked two metrics across the next eight hires: 90-day manager satisfaction scores and time-to-independence on their first major initiative.\n\nThe trade-off I accepted: The ambiguity exercise adds 45 minutes to an already long loop and increases candidate drop-off slightly. I decided that was worth it at the Senior PM level, though I'd reconsider for junior roles where ambiguity tolerance is less critical.\n\nOver the following year, I made six Senior PM hires using the revised process. Zero required a performance plan within the first year. That's not proof the process is perfect-sample size is small-but it's directional validation that the change mattered.\n\nThe lesson: don't just decide to be more careful. Change the instrument.",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "domain": "edtech",
    "tracks": [
      "Product Operations",
      "Internal Tools PM",
      "B2B / Enterprise PM"
    ]
