    "id": "dir-beh-11",
    "q": "Describe a time you influenced a major strategic decision at the executive level.",
    "a": "At the executive level, influence requires surfacing the right tension at the right moment with credible data.\n\nDuring annual planning, leadership was set to double down on enterprise after a strong quarter. I had conflicting signals: SMB cohort retention at 24 months was running 15 points higher, and SMB CAC was 40% lower. Enterprise growth looked compelling on ACV, but I suspected it was masking a structurally weaker business.\n\nFirst, I validated the data. I cross-referenced cohort retention across two data sources and had Finance confirm the CAC methodology before presenting anything. I needed to be certain the signal was real, not a segmentation artifact.\n\nI secured 30 minutes with the CPO before the planning session-not to lobby, but to ensure the analysis was visible before positions hardened. In session, I framed it as a strategic tension, not a recommendation: enterprise wins on ACV and brand; SMB wins on retention and unit economics. I proposed a 90-day pilot allocating 20% of incremental headcount to SMB, with explicit success metrics: SMB net revenue retention above 110%, CAC payback under 18 months, and pipeline coverage at 3x.\n\nThe team adopted a dual-track strategy with defined investment thresholds per segment. SMB investment increased by roughly 30%.\n\nThe trade-off I named explicitly: SMB deprioritization risked losing compounding retention advantages; enterprise over-investment risked deteriorating unit economics. Making that tension visible-rather than advocating a side-is what created room for a better decision.",
