    "id": "dir-beh-12",
    "q": "How have you managed your own professional development as a senior leader?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Feedback loops, deliberate stretch, and meta-skill compounding.\n\nI manage development across three vectors, each with explicit measurement.\n\nStructured feedback. Every six months I run a targeted 360 with 8 people-direct reports, peers, skip-levels. Three questions only: more of, less of, one blind spot. I track themes across cycles to distinguish signal from noise. When two consecutive cycles surfaced \"you move too fast in ambiguous situations,\" that became my explicit development target, not a vague intention.\n\nDeliberate exposure. I volunteer for problems outside my domain. Joining a cost-reduction initiative as the product representative felt uncomfortable-that was the point. I tracked a specific outcome: could I credibly challenge a finance assumption within 90 days? I could. That financial literacy has improved investment conversation outcomes measurably since.\n\nMeta-skill investment. I prioritize communication, systems thinking, and organizational design because they compound across every domain. Executive writing-tight one-pagers that drive decisions-has higher ROI than deepening any single product vertical.\n\nTrade-off I'm honest about: deliberate exposure creates short-term capacity strain. Taking on cross-functional work means something else gets less attention. I explicitly negotiate that with my manager rather than quietly absorbing the cost.\n\nValidation: I review whether my development bets are working by asking annually whether my influence on organizational decisions has expanded. That's the real metric at senior levels-not skill acquisition, but organizational impact.",
