    "q": "How do you set the standard for excellence in product management across a large product organization?",
    "a": "Setting standards at scale requires institutional mechanisms, not personal example. Here's my framework:\n\n1. Define excellence behaviorally, by level. Most orgs lack written, specific descriptions of excellent PM work at each level. Without this, every manager calibrates differently, creating inconsistency in feedback and promotions. I write this, review it annually, and make it broadly accessible.\n\n2. Validate the standard before scaling. Before rolling out org-wide, I test whether the rubric produces consistent assessments-having 5-6 managers independently evaluate the same work sample. If inter-rater variance exceeds 20%, the standard isn't specific enough. Fix it first.\n\n3. Create cross-PM visibility. Showcase sessions where PMs present a recent decision and reasoning, shared post-launch review templates, and portfolio-wide outcome summaries. Peer exposure is the most underrated calibration mechanism.\n\n4. Certify managers through calibration workshops. First-line managers are the standard's delivery mechanism. I run quarterly sessions where managers assess identical work samples independently, then reconcile. Manager variance is the leading indicator of downstream PM inconsistency.\n\n5. Run a promotion transparency pilot. For six months, publish a one-paragraph rationale for every PM promotion-specific behaviors and outcomes demonstrated. Measure whether self-assessed readiness scores improve and promotion surprise rates drop.\n\n6. Audit outcomes annually. If quality varies systematically by manager tenure or team size, that's a diagnostic signal, not random noise.\n\nTrade-off: This creates overhead and risks bureaucratic rigidity. The mitigation is annual rubric reviews that incorporate new inputs and retire outdated ones.",
