    "q": "How do you develop and communicate a product thesis to external audiences  . investors, partners, and the press?",
    "a": "Framework: Market Truth → Thesis Prediction → Audience Calibration → Narrative Governance\n\nA strong external product thesis inspires confidence without exposing competitive intent. It's a prediction, not a feature list.\n\nBuild it in two layers:\nStart with market truth: \"Here's an important, verifiable shift in how this market works.\" Then make a specific bet: \"We're uniquely positioned to capture the resulting opportunity because X.\" Example: \"Enterprise software is moving from seat-based tools to workflow-integrated systems-we're building the workflow layer, not another tool.\" That's a thesis. \"We build great enterprise products\" is not.\n\nCalibrate by audience:\n- Investors: market size evidence, team execution signal, leading traction indicators, multi-year durability\n- Partners: mutual value clarity, explicit scope boundaries\n- Press: problem/solution narrative, real user examples, a hook worth covering\n\nValidate before publishing: Test the thesis with 3-5 target audience members before broad distribution. Does the market prediction land as credible? Does the differentiation feel earned? Adjust based on where confidence breaks down.\n\nPilot: Before a major funding round or product launch, A/B test two thesis framings with warm investor contacts. Measure: meeting conversion rate, follow-up question quality, unprompted thesis recall in follow-up emails.\n\nMaintain narrative governance: Keep a timestamped record of key external claims. Inconsistency across 18-month touchpoints erodes trust fast.\n\nCore trade-off: Specificity builds credibility but narrows optionality. Define your \"won't say\" list before every external engagement and brief every spokesperson on it.",
