    "q": "How do you approach succession planning for key product roles?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Succession planning requires a proactive system, not a reactive scramble. I use a three-layer framework: capability mapping, pipeline development, and knowledge transfer infrastructure.\n\nFirst, validate the actual risk. I audit which roles would create critical path failures if vacated tomorrow. Typically 20% of roles carry 80% of the succession risk-usually principal PMs and directors owning core revenue lines. This data shapes where I invest development resources.\n\nSecond, build a forward-looking capability profile. Each key role gets a 2-3 year skills projection-not what the job requires today, but what product complexity, AI tooling, and business evolution will demand. Successors are developed against that future profile.\n\nThird, run structured development pilots. I identify candidates 2-3 levels below the role and assign 90-day stretch sprints: cross-functional initiative ownership, P&L accountability, or external partner management. I treat this as an A/B test-comparing development velocity of candidates with structured assignments versus those receiving only mentorship conversations.\n\nMetrics I track: time-to-readiness per candidate, internal fill rate for key roles (target: 70%+), and retention of high-potentials after succession conversations.\n\nTrade-offs to acknowledge: Transparent succession conversations accelerate development but create flight risk if timelines slip. Overcommunicating creates expectation debt. I default to honesty about development trajectory while staying deliberately vague on specific timelines-preserving optionality without signaling lack of confidence.",
