    "q": "How do you manage a product portfolio across multiple lines with competing investment priorities?",
    "a": "Framework: Stage-Role Matrix with Pre-Committed Exit Criteria\n\nI manage portfolios by mapping each product on two axes: lifecycle stage (explore/scale/harvest) and strategic role (growth driver, retention anchor, moat builder). This prevents the common failure of negotiation-based allocation where every team gets something and nothing gets enough.\n\nBefore finalizing allocation, I validate the map with data. I audit each product's actual contribution metrics-revenue growth rate, retention impact, competitive differentiation score-against its stated strategic role. Mismatches surface immediately and force honest reclassification.\n\nResource allocation follows the map: Explore-stage products receive time-boxed budgets (typically 10-15% of portfolio) with 90-day decision gates. Scale-stage products receive the majority allocation. Harvest-stage products receive efficiency investment only-no growth headcount.\n\nTo test allocation assumptions, I run structured pilots. For example, if two products compete for scale-stage investment, I'd run a 60-day A/B test: one receives full growth investment, the other maintenance-only, then compare CAC efficiency, NRR movement, and feature adoption velocity.\n\nSuccess metrics: portfolio-level NRR, revenue concentration risk (no single product >60% of portfolio revenue), and exit-criteria hit rate per product.\n\nThe core trade-off: this model requires saying no to teams with legitimate roadmaps. That creates organizational friction. I mitigate it by publishing the framework transparently so decisions feel principled, not political-but the friction is real and shouldn't be underestimated.",
