    "q": "What's your process for integrating a newly acquired product into your portfolio?",
    "a": "Framework: Thesis → Audit → Model → Validate → Execute\n\nBefore touching roadmap or architecture, I lock down the acquisition thesis-users, technology, team, or market position-because that single decision dictates integration speed, what to preserve, and what to sunset.\n\n30-day audit first. I validate the pitch deck assumptions against reality: actual MAU versus reported, real technical debt load, NPS of the acquired product, and cultural distance from our engineering org. Acquisitions fail when integration plans are built on seller narratives rather than ground truth.\n\nExplicit integration model decision at day 31: full absorption, portfolio standalone, or arm's-length independence. I run a lightweight A/B test where possible-for example, exposing 10% of our existing users to the acquired feature before full integration-measuring activation rate, retention delta, and support ticket volume to validate the thesis before committing engineering resources.\n\nSuccess metrics I track: 90-day user retention of the acquired product post-announcement, cross-product feature adoption rate, integration engineering velocity (story points delivered versus baseline), and acquired team retention at 6 and 12 months.\n\nTrade-off I make explicit: speed versus preservation. Fast integration reduces operational overhead but risks destroying the differentiated behavior that made the acquisition valuable. Slow integration preserves product DNA but bleeds cost and creates org confusion.\n\nOne dedicated integration PM with explicit decision rights is non-negotiable. Diffuse ownership is the single most common reason acquisitions underdeliver.",
