    "q": "How do you set company-level product OKRs that cascade effectively through the organization?",
    "a": "Framework: Outcome Hierarchy with Bidirectional Alignment\n\nStart by establishing 3-5 company-level outcome OKRs tied directly to strategic bets-\"increase enterprise retention by 8 points\" not \"ship 12 features.\" Activity OKRs make teams busy; outcome OKRs make teams effective.\n\nBefore finalizing OKRs, validate the data: Audit whether current instrumentation can actually measure the proposed key results. I've seen companies commit to retention OKRs while lacking cohort-level churn visibility. If you can't measure it today, either fix the instrumentation first or choose a different KR.\n\nCascade mechanism: Run bidirectional alignment-company OKRs set direction, teams draft their own OKRs showing explicit contribution lines upward. Any team that can't draw that connection is working on misaligned priorities. This surfaces organizational blind spots before the quarter starts.\n\nPilot the review cadence: In Q1, A/B test monthly health checks (are we on-track, what's blocking) against teams using quarterly-only reviews. Measure decision velocity and OKR completion rates. Monthly cadence consistently outperforms in fast-moving environments.\n\nSuccess metrics: OKR completion rate >70%, <20% OKRs revised mid-quarter, team-to-company alignment coverage at 100%.\n\nTrade-off: Bidirectional OKR setting takes 2-3x longer than top-down assignment. The payoff is genuine ownership versus compliance. Designate 1-2 explicit \"strategic bet\" OKRs annually for long-horizon investments where short-term metrics will look flat-protecting these from quarterly pressure is essential.",
