    "q": "How do you approach pricing strategy as a product leader  . what's your framework?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Pricing is one of the highest-leverage product decisions-a 5% price increase typically outperforms a 5% volume increase in margin impact, yet most product teams abdicate ownership to finance or sales.\n\nMy framework has four stages:\n\n1. Identify the value metric first. What unit correlates directly with value delivered? Seats for collaboration tools, consumption for infrastructure, outcomes for results-driven products. The wrong value metric misaligns incentives permanently.\n\n2. Validate willingness-to-pay before deciding. I run Van Westendorp or conjoint analysis during discovery-not post-launch. This surfaces real price sensitivity curves segmented by user type, since enterprise and SMB ceilings differ dramatically.\n\n3. Pilot with a controlled A/B test. Before full rollout, I test pricing changes on a matched cohort-typically 10-15% of new users-measuring conversion rate, time-to-close, expansion revenue, and 90-day retention. This catches elasticity surprises before they compound.\n\n4. Model three-year compounding effects. A 5% price increase looks modest quarterly; factored across retention curves and expansion motion, it reshapes unit economics materially.\n\nTrade-offs I explicitly hold: Usage-based pricing maximizes adoption but creates revenue unpredictability-critical to flag with finance early. Outcome-based pricing builds trust but requires defensible attribution, which many products can't deliver yet.\n\nSuccess metrics: ARR per customer segment, logo retention, NPS by price tier, and sales cycle length. Pricing is a product feature-I own it accordingly.",
