    "q": "What's your framework for deciding whether to build, buy, or partner for a major capability?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Differentiation × Speed × Risk\n\nBefore deciding, I validate three data points: (1) does this capability appear in win/loss interviews as a purchase driver, (2) what's our internal build estimate versus market cost, and (3) what's the competitive half-life-how long before parity erodes any advantage?\n\nBuild when the capability is a proven purchase driver and you have 18+ months of runway to execute. Control over the roadmap compounds into durable differentiation. Buy when the opportunity window is shorter than your build timeline and the acquisition price buys team, data, or market position-not just features. Partner when the capability is adjacent, a specialist maintains it better, and you can negotiate favorable terms including an exit clause and a fallback build plan.\n\nThe non-negotiable rule: never accept vendor dependency on a differentiating capability. Commodity functions can tolerate it; your core identity cannot.\n\nPilot approach: For any borderline build-vs-partner decision, I'd run a 90-day technical spike in parallel with a partner integration. Success metrics are time-to-feature-parity, P75 latency, integration incident rate, and fully-loaded cost per unit. The spike reveals hidden complexity; the partner integration reveals hidden dependency.\n\nTrade-off I'd name explicitly: buying accelerates speed-to-market but caps long-term margin and roadmap control. That trade is worth making in year one; it usually isn't in year three when usage data and team capability can support a superior internal solution.",
