    "q": "How do you define and measure product-market fit for a mature enterprise product?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "For mature enterprise products, PMF isn't binary-it's a defense and expansion problem. I measure it across four dimensions with clear thresholds.\n\nCore metrics:\n- Retention depth: >90% 12-month retention segmented by role (admin, power user, casual). Flat aggregate retention masking power-user churn is a critical early warning.\n- Net Revenue Retention: >120% signals expanding value; <100% means churn outpaces expansion-act immediately.\n- Competitive win rate: <50% in your historically dominant segment indicates fit erosion, tracked quarterly by competitor and vertical.\n- Advocacy score: <40% \"very disappointed\" response in your core segment is a warning threshold; I track NPS trajectory, not just snapshot.\n\nValidation step: Before acting on any single metric, I cross-reference all four. Stable retention with declining NPS and shrinking deal sizes is more dangerous than any single drop-it signals slow erosion competitors will exploit.\n\nPilot approach: If NRR trends below 115%, I'd run a structured win/loss A/B analysis-interviewing 20 churned vs. 20 retained accounts quarterly to isolate whether fit issues are product gaps, pricing, or competitive displacement.\n\nTrade-off: Optimizing NRR can incentivize upsell pressure that damages advocacy scores long-term. I'd weight retention depth and advocacy as leading indicators, NRR as lagging confirmation.\n\nThe most dangerous PMF signal isn't a sharp decline-it's slow drift across multiple dimensions simultaneously.",
