    "q": "How do you structure the relationship between product, engineering, and design at the Senior Director level?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "At Senior Director level, your job is designing the system that makes product-engineering-design relationships work at scale-not managing individual team dynamics.\n\nThe model I use: Shared outcome ownership with clear decision rights.\n\nFirst, I validate whether the current structure is broken by looking at specific signals: cycle time on cross-functional decisions, how often conflicts escalate to me, and whether post-mortems reveal misaligned incentives. If separate OKRs exist per function, that's usually the root cause-you get optimized handoffs instead of integrated problem-solving.\n\nThree structural changes that fix this:\n\n1. Shared OKRs at the triad level. Product, engineering, and design leaders co-own outcomes and are evaluated together. This alone eliminates 60% of escalations I've seen.\n\n2. Parallel triad planning. Product strategy, engineering architecture, and design systems developed simultaneously each quarter-not sequentially. I run explicit \"triad kickoffs\" before individual roadmaps are drafted.\n\n3. Explicit decision rights. Product owns \"what and why,\" engineering owns \"how,\" design owns \"user experience.\" Intersection conflicts resolved with data; escalate to me only when triad alignment fails after 48 hours.\n\nTo validate the model works: I'd run a 90-day pilot with one high-priority triad, measuring decision cycle time, escalation rate, and team NPS. If cycle time drops 25%+ and escalations decrease, I roll it out broadly.\n\nKey trade-off: Shared accountability requires psychological safety. If the culture punishes failure, this model backfires-leaders will avoid co-ownership to protect themselves.",
