    "q": "How do you approach international expansion from a product strategy perspective?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "International expansion fails most often not from lack of ambition but from premature commitment-entering before validating that your core value proposition survives the market translation.\n\nMy framework: Validate, Focus, Build, Staff.\n\nValidate first. Before any roadmap investment, I look for organic signals-unprompted signups from the target market, inbound support requests in local language, or partnership inquiries. If organic pull exists, I run structured qualitative research (15-20 interviews) to confirm the problem is structurally similar, not just superficially familiar.\n\nFocus on winnable markets. I score candidates on three axes: organic demand signal, competitive fragmentation, and our ability to iterate locally. Markets where we can realistically capture meaningful share within 18 months beat aspirational logos every time.\n\nRun a staged pilot before full commitment. I'd propose a 90-day limited-availability launch with a waitlist, measuring activation rate, 30-day retention, and NPS versus our baseline market. If retention is within 15% of home market, we proceed. If not, we diagnose before scaling.\n\nBuild for localization depth, not just translation. The real product investment is payments, compliance, and local UX patterns-not string replacement.\n\nThe core trade-off: moving fast risks building for a phantom user; moving slow cedes ground to local competitors. The pilot structure resolves this tension by generating real behavioral data before we've staffed, localized, and committed a full roadmap cycle to a market that may not convert.",
