    "q": "What's your approach to managing technical debt at the organizational level  . when to invest and how much?",
    "a": "Technical debt management is a capital allocation decision. I treat it with the same rigor as feature investment.\n\nFramework:\n\nClassify by business impact, not engineering aesthetics. Three tiers: debt blocking user-facing reliability (P1), debt degrading engineering velocity (P2), purely architectural debt (P3). P1 gets immediate resourcing; P2 gets steady-state allocation; P3 competes normally.\n\nReserve 15-20% engineering capacity for debt reduction. Before changing that baseline, I validate with data: measure deployment frequency, incident rate, and feature cycle time quarterly. When capacity drops below 10%, these metrics historically degrade within 2-3 quarters-that's the evidence I bring to leadership.\n\nRequire ROI cases for major refactors. A 6 engineer-month platform rewrite needs explicit expected returns: 30% faster feature delivery, 40% incident reduction, specific dollar savings. Without this, debt investment competes on sentiment rather than evidence.\n\nPilot before committing. For large architectural changes, I propose a bounded A/B approach: refactor one service or module first, then measure deployment frequency, error rates, and developer productivity against baseline before scaling the investment.\n\nDefine debt ceilings proactively. Some debt compounds exponentially. I surface these early: \"If we don't address authentication infrastructure this quarter, onboarding new enterprise customers becomes 3x more expensive.\"\n\nThe trade-off I acknowledge: Higher debt allocation slows near-term feature output. The PM's job is translating engineering concerns into business impact-making that trade-off explicit rather than letting engineering carry the political cost alone.",
