    "q": "How do you approach data strategy as a product leader?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "My data strategy framework operates across three layers: user value, product intelligence, and competitive moat - but strategy without validation is just theory.\n\nLayer 1 - User-facing value: I map which data inputs drive which product experiences (personalization, benchmarks, recommendations), then identify under-utilized signals. Before building, I validate assumptions with a structured pilot: instrument one feature with enhanced data inputs, A/B test the experience against a control, and measure retention delta, engagement depth, and NPS movement over 30 days.\n\nLayer 2 - Product intelligence: I audit instrumentation annually against a simple test: can every team answer their top three product questions without a data team ticket? Key metrics: query self-service rate, experiment velocity (tests shipped per quarter), and funnel measurement coverage.\n\nLayer 3 - Competitive moat: Network-effect data compounds; commodity data doesn't. I assess what we hold that competitors can't replicate cheaply, then invest disproportionately there.\n\nGovernance reality check: Privacy compliance, data quality monitoring, and broad internal access aren't overhead - poor data quality is invisible product debt that corrupts every downstream decision.\n\nTrade-offs I navigate consciously: Collecting more data improves intelligence but increases privacy risk and maintenance burden. Broad data access accelerates decisions but creates governance exposure. Moat-building requires upfront investment that competes with near-term feature work.\n\nThe discipline is prioritizing data investments by expected compounding value, not collection completeness.",
