    "q": "How do you build a product research function that scales with the organization?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Build research as organizational infrastructure, not a service bureau.\n\nStructure the function across three layers: professional researchers own generative/strategic work, PMs and designers run evaluative research with lightweight tooling, and shared infrastructure (panels, repositories, synthesis tools) serves everyone. This prevents both bottlenecks and duplication.\n\nValidate first. Before scaling headcount, audit how current research is actually used. If fewer than 60% of completed studies influenced a product decision, the constraint is adoption, not capacity. Fix the feedback loop before adding researchers.\n\nPilot: Run a 90-day \"continuous discovery\" experiment with two product teams. Embed weekly 30-minute user conversations into their standard operating rhythm, tracked in a shared repository. Measure against a control group of teams using project-gated research. Success metrics: decision cycle time, percentage of roadmap items with supporting research, and post-launch NPS delta between teams.\n\nKey metrics at scale: research-to-decision conversion rate (target >70%), time-from-question-to-insight (target <5 days for evaluative, <3 weeks for generative), and repository reuse rate before initiating new studies.\n\nCore trade-off: Distributed research increases velocity but risks quality inconsistency. Mitigate by centralizing methodology standards and researcher review, not execution. Pair qual researchers with data scientists structurally-quant surfaces the \"what,\" qual explains the \"why\"-so neither function operates blind.\n\nThe scaling unlock is making research easy to access before making it easy to produce.",
