    "q": "What's your framework for building a product culture in an organization that didn't previously have strong PM practice?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Building product culture is a change management challenge first, a hiring challenge second. My framework has five stages, but the sequence matters.\n\nStart with diagnosis, not prescription. Before introducing PM process, I audit how decisions currently get made: who has authority, where user feedback enters (or doesn't), and what's celebrated at all-hands. This data prevents imposing solutions that don't fit the org's actual failure modes.\n\nEarn trust through visible wins. In the first 90 days, I identify one concrete problem-typically a roadmap prioritization dispute or a shipped feature with poor adoption-and solve it using PM methods. The outcome makes the case better than any framework presentation.\n\nBuild shared language before shared process. I run cross-functional workshops on \"user problem vs. solution,\" \"hypothesis,\" and \"success metric\" with engineering and design included, not just PMs. Language alignment accelerates everything downstream.\n\nRun a controlled pilot. I propose a 60-day A/B test: one team operating under the new PM model, one under the existing model. Success metrics are decision speed, feature adoption at 30 days, and engineer satisfaction scores. This surfaces friction without org-wide disruption.\n\nHire and celebrate correctly. PMs promoted for shipping fast regardless of outcomes anchor the wrong culture. I track which behaviors leadership recognizes publicly and correct misalignment explicitly.\n\nTrade-off awareness: Moving slower early-discovery, research visibility, workshops-creates friction with engineering leads. The pilot structure addresses this by making the value measurable before asking for full commitment.",
