    "q": "How do you approach competitive strategy at the product level  . how do you respond to a well-funded competitor?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Assess → Differentiate → Defend, across three time horizons.\n\nBefore responding to a well-funded competitor, I run a rapid diagnostic: Are they winning in our core segment or an adjacent one? Is their feature a genuine capability gap or a marketing move? I validate this with data-churn interviews, win/loss analysis, and support ticket themes-before touching the roadmap. Reactive feature-matching is almost always the wrong answer.\n\nImmediate (0-3 months): If data confirms a real gap, I run a targeted A/B test-not a full build. Ship a lightweight version to 10-15% of at-risk accounts, measure 30-day retention delta and NPS against control. If retention improves by 5%+, we accelerate. If not, we've avoided a costly distraction.\n\nMedium-term (6-18 months): I invest where we have structural advantages they can't easily replicate-proprietary data, deep integrations, workflow lock-in. These create switching costs that outlast any feature war.\n\nLong-term: The durable answer is redefining the game. I ask: what problem space can we own so completely that challenging us requires them to restructure their strategy?\n\nSuccess metrics: win rate in core segment, net revenue retention, competitive displacement rate, and time-to-close in contested deals.\n\nTrade-off I watch: Competitive pressure can manufacture urgency that crowds out foundational bets. I run quarterly competitive reviews to keep this systematic-so we're making strategic choices, not writing a response strategy.",
