    "q": "What is your framework for setting up experimentation at scale  . A/B testing, feature flags, and beyond?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Treat experimentation as infrastructure, not a process.\n\nMy north star: every team should be able to ship, measure, and roll back independently within 48 hours. I build toward that through four layers:\n\n1. Foundation - Feature Flag System\nAll significant changes live behind flags. This enables gradual rollouts (1%→10%→100%), instant rollback without deploys, and clean experiment assignment. Before scaling this, I validate adoption: are >80% of new features flag-gated? If not, the cultural problem precedes the tooling problem.\n\n2. Instrumentation Standardization\nEnforce a single event taxonomy with ownership. Without this, teams measure different things and results aren't comparable. Data validation step: audit event coverage quarterly; flag gaps before they corrupt experiment results.\n\n3. Statistical Rigor by Default\nPre-register primary metrics, power calculations, and minimum detectable effect before launch. Hard rule: no peeking before minimum runtime. This prevents the most common failure-stopping when results look good.\n\n4. Experiment Registry\nShared log of all live and completed experiments prevents collision, p-hacking, and duplicated work.\n\nPilot I'd propose: Run a structured A/B test on one high-traffic surface with pre-registered success metrics (primary: conversion/retention; guardrail: latency, error rate). Use results to calibrate your MDE assumptions before scaling.\n\nTrade-off: Rigorous experimentation slows feature velocity short-term. The counterargument-and it's correct-is that shipping unvalidated features at speed is just faster waste accumulation.",
