    "q": "How do you define your north star metric and what happens when it conflicts with near-term business metrics?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: North star metrics must be causal, not correlational, and conflicts with near-term metrics require explicit trade-off decisions, not avoidance.\n\nDefining the north star:\n1. Identify the core job-to-be-done - what outcome users actually hire the product to achieve\n2. Select the metric measuring job completion, not feature usage\n3. Validate statistically: does this metric predict 90-day retention and expansion revenue? If the correlation is weak, it's an interesting metric, not a north star\n\nWhen conflicts arise:\nConflicts almost always signal extractive optimization - monetizing attention rather than delivering value. My approach is to name the conflict explicitly and force a structured decision:\n\n- Quantify the short-term gain (e.g., $200K additional quarterly revenue from dark-pattern cancellation flow)\n- Model the downstream cost (increased churn rate, LTV erosion, NPS decline)\n- Run a 60-day A/B test: control group sees optimized cancellation flow, test group sees friction-reduced but value-reinforcing flow\n- Measure: 30-day reactivation rate, 6-month LTV, CSAT at cancellation\n\nTrade-off reality: Sometimes short-term business metrics must win - a company facing runway constraints can't defer revenue. The discipline is making that call consciously, with a defined timeline to reverse the compromise, not letting it become permanent product direction.\n\nThe north star isn't sacred. It's the primary accountability mechanism. Overriding it is a business decision that must be owned at CPO or CEO level, documented, and revisited quarterly.",
