    "q": "What's your approach to security and compliance as product requirements  . not as engineering afterthoughts?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Security and compliance are product quality attributes-identical in priority to performance or reliability. My framework treats them as first-class requirements from day one.\n\nFour structural commitments:\n\n1. Explicit trust model in the PRD. Every product spec defines who holds what data, under what expectations. Consumer, enterprise, and healthcare have fundamentally different trust contracts-this gets written down, not inferred.\n\n2. Security-gated definition of done. Features touching auth, payments, or PII require structured threat modeling before shipping. Not a checkbox-an actual review with documented risk acceptance.\n\n3. Living compliance roadmap. Regulatory changes (GDPR updates, state privacy laws, SOC2 scope expansion) get tracked alongside the product roadmap with explicit owner and ship date.\n\n4. Privacy budget discipline. Before any new data collection, I validate: does user value justify the regulatory exposure and trust cost? If the answer requires a spreadsheet to justify, it's probably no.\n\nData validation I'd add: Audit current feature backlog-what percentage of items have security review scheduled versus shipped without one? That gap quantifies actual risk exposure.\n\nPilot: For the next quarter, run an A/B on security-review-at-spec vs. security-review-at-code-complete. Measure post-launch vulnerability findings and remediation cost. I'd expect 40%+ reduction in late-stage security rework.\n\nSuccess metrics: Zero P0 security findings at launch, compliance roadmap coverage at 100%, privacy budget sign-off rate tracked per team.\n\nTrade-off: This slows initial velocity 10-15% but eliminates the catastrophic velocity loss from post-launch incidents.",
