    "q": "How do you build a PM team that can execute on both product discovery and product delivery simultaneously?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Dual-Track Operating Model with Explicit Capacity Governance\n\nFirst, I'd validate whether the team actually has a discovery deficit or a prioritization problem-by auditing the last six quarters: what percentage of shipped features moved target metrics? If under 40%, the issue is discovery quality. If over 60% of work was unplanned reactive work, the issue is delivery discipline. The intervention differs.\n\nAssuming genuine dual-track failure, I'd implement three structural changes:\n\nCapacity split: Explicitly allocate 25% PM time to discovery 1-2 quarters ahead, protected from sprint pressure. Without governance, delivery consumes everything.\n\nDiscovery exit criteria: Before engineering picks up any problem, require: validated problem statement, quantitative signal (usage data or survey), one falsified assumption, and a measurable success metric. This eliminates discovery theater.\n\nEmbedded data partnership: Pair each PM with a dedicated data scientist-not a request queue. Qualitative interviews explain why; quantitative analysis confirms at scale. Both are required.\n\nPilot: Run a 90-day A/B test across two comparable pod pairs-one with structured dual-track cadence, one status quo. Measure: feature adoption rate at 30 days, time-from-discovery-to-validated-spec, and PM-reported confidence scores pre-launch.\n\nTrade-off awareness: The 25% discovery allocation creates short-term delivery velocity pressure. That's acceptable if leadership has visibility and agrees the cost of building wrong features exceeds the cost of slower output-which the audit data should confirm before committing.",
