    "q": "How do you think about product velocity  . what actually makes teams ship faster without sacrificing quality?",
    "a": "Velocity is a systems problem, not a hustle problem. My framework: diagnose the constraint before prescribing a solution.\n\nBefore changing anything, I run a two-week velocity audit. I measure cycle time by stage (discovery → spec → build → review → deploy), identify where work sits idle, and calculate decision latency-the average hours waiting for approvals or unblocking answers. Most teams discover 60-70% of their slowdown lives outside coding entirely.\n\nThe five highest-leverage levers I've found:\n1. Problem clarity - vague specs cause mid-sprint rework; better discovery eliminates it\n2. Decision ownership - undefined approvers create the longest queues\n3. Active-path technical debt - only prioritize debt slowing current work, not theoretical cleanup\n4. Focus over parallelism - fewer simultaneous workstreams increases individual throughput\n5. Developer experience - CI/CD speed and test infrastructure have compounding ROI\n\nPilot I'd run: Take two comparable squads. Give one dedicated discovery time (20% of sprint) and explicit decision authority within their domain. Measure cycle time, rework rate, and escaped defects over six sprints.\n\nTrade-off to acknowledge: Investing in discovery and DX slows output short-term. Leadership needs to accept a 4-6 week dip before velocity compounds. Skipping this conversation is why most velocity initiatives fail.\n\nSuccess metrics: cycle time reduction, rework rate, deploy frequency, and team-reported decision latency.",
