    "q": "What's your framework for managing a product through a significant economic downturn?",
    "a": "Framework: Triage across three zones-cut, protect, and attack.\n\nFirst, I validate the actual pressure: ARR contraction rate, logo churn velocity, and customer health scores across segments. This tells me whether we're in a revenue defense crisis or still have strategic headroom. Data first-gut reactions in downturns are almost always wrong.\n\nCut: Growth bets with uncertain, long-dated returns. Features driving press over retention. I run a ruthless audit: if a feature disappeared tomorrow, would a paying customer churn? If no, it's a candidate for the cut list.\n\nProtect: The retention core-features CS highlights in renewal conversations. Churn prevention has the highest ROI of any activity in a downturn. I'd A/B test messaging and packaging changes here specifically, measuring renewal rate lift and NRR improvement within 90 days before committing engineering resources.\n\nAttack: Competitors are cutting. Customers are more price-sensitive and more open to switching. I identify 1-2 investments that widen our cost-value advantage while others retreat-targeting their churning accounts.\n\nTrade-offs I'm explicit about: Protecting retention core means delaying net-new acquisition features, which hurts pipeline recovery speed. Attacking opportunistically requires capital discipline elsewhere. The risk is over-indexing on survival and exiting the downturn with a smaller, more defensive product.\n\nSuccess metrics: NRR stabilization within two quarters, logo churn below pre-downturn baseline, and competitive win rate improvement in the recovery period.",
