    "q": "How do you manage the product organization through leadership transitions  . when your key leaders leave?",
    "a": "Framework: Stabilize → Validate → Rebuild\n\nLeadership transitions fail when organizations conflate urgency with speed. My approach separates immediate stabilization from long-term structural repair.\n\nImmediate (Week 1-2): Appoint an interim lead-typically the strongest senior IC-and conduct a rapid knowledge audit. I specifically map: active stakeholder commitments, in-flight roadmap dependencies, and undocumented strategic rationale. This isn't optional; I've seen transitions derail entirely because a departing leader held critical context about *why* a decision was made, not just what it was.\n\nValidation before hiring: Before posting the role, I run a 2-week diagnostic-surveying the team on capability gaps, reviewing OKR performance trends, and interviewing cross-functional partners. The departing leader's profile is often the wrong template for their successor as the product evolves.\n\nPilot the interim structure: Run the interim arrangement for 60-90 days as a deliberate experiment. Measure: team velocity (story points/sprint), PM retention rate, stakeholder satisfaction scores (quarterly NPS), and roadmap delivery against commitments. This data validates whether the gap is structural or purely personnel.\n\nTrade-offs I acknowledge: Moving slowly on the hire preserves quality but risks team attrition-top PMs have options. I set a hard deadline of 90 days maximum for interim arrangements to prevent organizational drift.\n\nPrevention investment: Mandatory succession planning for all Director+ roles annually, with documented decision frameworks and explicit \"second owner\" assignments for every critical workstream.",
