    "q": "What is your process for managing and communicating product strategy shifts to the board?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Signal → Shift → Success Criteria → Risk\n\nBefore presenting any strategy shift, I validate the signal. Is this a true market inflection or internal noise? I require three independent data sources-customer churn analysis, competitive movement, and revenue cohort trends-before declaring a strategic shift warranted.\n\nCommunication structure for the board:\n\nLead with market evidence, not internal decisions. \"Segment X is contracting 15% YoY while adjacent segment Y is growing 40%\" lands better than \"we're pivoting.\" Boards respond to market logic, not product opinions.\n\nShow continuity explicitly. Map what assumptions held, what new data invalidated, and why the prior strategy was rational given what we knew. Shifts that look like reversals destroy confidence; evolutions build it.\n\nDefine measurable outcomes: specific metrics, targets, and timeframes. \"We'll validate this in 90 days if pilot cohort LTV exceeds $X and CAC stays below $Y.\"\n\nPilot discipline: Before the board meeting, I run a limited A/B test or 60-day pilot with 10-15% of the segment to generate real evidence-not projections. This transforms the conversation from \"trust us\" to \"here's early signal.\"\n\nPre-wire before the room: Share the framing with key board members individually beforehand. Board meetings are for decision-making, not first exposure.\n\nTrade-off I name explicitly: Moving fast on strategy shifts risks team whiplash and execution debt. I balance urgency against organizational absorption capacity and quantify that cost directly.",
