    "q": "How do you build and maintain strategic partnerships from a product perspective?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Evaluate partnerships on user value, then commercial fit, then execution depth.\n\nBefore pursuing any partnership, I validate the premise with data: Are users already seeking this capability (support tickets, workarounds, third-party integrations they've self-built)? If demand isn't proven, the partnership is a bet, not a strategy.\n\nBuilding partnerships:\n\n1. Define the joint value proposition in one sentence - if you can't articulate the user problem it solves, the partnership is too abstract to execute. Slack + Google Drive works because \"find and share files without leaving your workflow\" is crisp.\n\n2. Calibrate investment to strategic importance - critical partnerships get dedicated PM time, shared roadmap visibility, and joint GTM planning. Commodity integrations get lightweight, automated approaches with abstraction layers to enable easy swaps if the partner is acquired or terms change.\n\n3. Pilot before committing - run a beta with 5–10% of users measuring activation rate, feature adoption, and retention delta versus non-integrated users. Don't negotiate full commercial terms until the pilot proves user value.\n\nMaintaining partnerships:\n\nMeasure partnership health with real signals: API call volume, joint user activation, shared customer retention. A commercially signed partnership with low adoption is already failing - address it early or sunset it.\n\nKey trade-off: Deep integrations create switching costs that protect partnerships but create dependency risk. I weight that against strategic value and always build abstraction layers for critical integrations.",
