    "q": "How do you define and implement a design system strategy?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "A design system is shared infrastructure-it produces no direct user value but determines how fast and consistently everything else gets built. My strategy has five pillars:\n\nEstablish executive sponsorship first. A design system advocated only by design loses every prioritization battle. I position it as engineering infrastructure and secure representation in roadmap planning at the VP level or above.\n\nValidate scope with data before building. I audit existing UI components across products, identify the top 20% of patterns covering 80% of surfaces, and measure current inconsistency costs-duplicate components, rework hours, accessibility failures. This baseline justifies investment and defines the MVP.\n\nPilot with one high-traffic product team. I run a controlled rollout: one team adopts the system for 60 days while a comparable team continues without it. Success metrics: component build time (target: 40% reduction), design-to-dev handoff cycles, and defect rates on UI components.\n\nDefine governance explicitly. Contribution model must allow product teams to propose patterns while a core team maintains quality gates-otherwise the system either stagnates or becomes inconsistent.\n\nVersion like software. Breaking changes get deprecation timelines, migration guides, and adoption tracking. I treat design system changes with the same rigor as breaking API changes.\n\nTrade-offs to acknowledge: Centralized systems create bottlenecks if under-resourced. Over-standardization slows teams building differentiated experiences. The system should constrain the common cases and stay silent on the exceptions.",
