    "q": "How do you approach go-to-market strategy as a product leader  . what's your role vs. marketing and sales?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Product owns the strategy layer of GTM; marketing and sales own execution. When these boundaries blur, launches fail-not because the product is weak, but because accountability is diffuse.\n\nMy framework:\n\nProduct owns: positioning (we know the user deepest), packaging and tiering, activation/onboarding, and launch readiness checklists including instrumentation and support docs.\n\nProduct influences but doesn't own: demand generation (I provide the product narrative; marketing crafts campaigns) and sales enablement (I build battle cards and demos; sales leadership deploys them).\n\nBefore any launch, I validate assumptions with data. I interview 10–15 target customers to pressure-test our value proposition, then audit activation funnels to confirm we're solving the right friction points-not launching into a broken onboarding experience.\n\nI run a structured pilot before full rollout. For a new tier or feature launch, I'll A/B test two positioning variants with a 5–10% user cohort, measuring 30-day activation rate, feature adoption depth, and expansion revenue signal before committing to broad GTM execution.\n\nSuccess metrics I track: time-to-value (sign-up to first key action), activation rate at day-7 and day-30, NPS segmented by acquisition channel, and sales cycle length as a proxy for positioning clarity.\n\nThe core trade-off: investing PM time in GTM means less time on roadmap. I accept that trade-off deliberately-a great product with weak GTM loses to an average product with sharp GTM. Users don't segment their experience by org chart.",
