    "q": "What is your approach to feature deprecation  . when and how do you remove functionality?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Deprecate by ROI, not by politics.\n\nBefore any removal decision, I validate three data points: active usage (last 90 days, not all-time), support cost attribution, and substitutability. If a feature has <2% MAU, generates disproportionate support tickets, and has a functional equivalent, it's a deprecation candidate. If any dimension is uncertain, I run a 30-day instrumentation sprint before deciding.\n\nDecision criteria I set at launch, not retroactively:\nEvery feature brief includes a deprecation trigger-specific usage and value thresholds that automatically surface it for review. This removes the politics of ad-hoc removal debates.\n\nPilot before full removal:\nI'll A/B test deprecation with 10% of users-hide the feature, measure support volume, churn signal, and workflow abandonment. If impact is negligible, proceed. If not, I've learned cheaply.\n\nMigration and notice windows:\n- Minor features: 90-day notice, in-product banner\n- Workflow-critical features: 6+ months, direct outreach, migration tooling\n\nSuccess metrics: reduction in codebase complexity (proxy: engineering cycle time), support ticket deflection, and zero spike in churn during sunset window.\n\nThe trade-off I explicitly acknowledge: aggressive deprecation accelerates velocity but risks alienating power users who rely on niche functionality. I mitigate by segmenting-if a feature has low breadth but high depth among a high-LTV cohort, the calculus changes entirely. Revenue retention trumps code cleanliness.",
