    "q": "How do you manage the tension between long-term platform investment and short-term feature delivery?",
    "subcategory": "product_design",
    "a": "Framework: Treat platform investment as a fixed tax, not a discretionary budget.\n\nI allocate 20% of engineering capacity to platform work as a non-negotiable standing commitment-protected in quarterly planning before feature prioritization begins. This prevents platform from losing every short-term tradeoff.\n\nBefore locking that rate, I validate the actual cost of underinvestment. I measure deployment frequency, incident rate tied to platform gaps, and feature cycle time. If teams are spending >30% of sprint capacity on workarounds, the 20% rate is likely too low.\n\nI then distinguish three platform layers with different investment models: foundational infrastructure (reliability, security), developer capabilities (APIs, shared services), and shared surfaces (design systems). Each requires separate ROI framing-foundational work reduces incident cost, developer capabilities compress feature cycle time.\n\nTo justify the investment organizationally, I translate platform work into feature delivery language: \"This pipeline investment reduces deployment time 60%, enabling 15 additional feature releases annually.\" I also surface explicit feature blockers created by platform gaps-teams waiting on platform capabilities create organizational urgency better than any roadmap slide.\n\nPilot approach: Run a two-quarter experiment holding platform investment at 20%, measuring feature throughput, deployment frequency, and unplanned platform work as a percentage of total engineering time. Compare against prior two quarters.\n\nThe core tradeoff: Higher platform rates slow near-term feature velocity but compound positively. Lower rates accelerate short-term delivery while accumulating debt that eventually forces a painful, disruptive replatforming.",
