    "q": "What metrics do you use to evaluate PM performance at scale?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Evaluate PMs on outcomes, process, craft, and influence-weighted by role scope and time horizon.\n\nBefore applying this, I validate that each PM has a measurable baseline: defined OKRs, documented decisions, and at least one full launch cycle. Without that, the evaluation is opinion.\n\nFour dimensions I score:\n\n1. Outcome quality: Did key product metrics move in the intended direction? I adjust for external factors by comparing against market benchmarks and control cohorts. Long-horizon bets get scored on leading indicators instead.\n\n2. Process rigor: Were hypotheses tested before building? Did launches ship with pre-defined success criteria? I audit three to five decision artifacts per cycle.\n\n3. Craft: Are briefs, specs, and strategy memos clear enough to reduce back-and-forth? I measure rework requests from engineering and design as a proxy.\n\n4. Cross-functional trust: Structured 360s from eng, design, and data-scored numerically, not qualitatively, so I can track trends.\n\nTo pilot this: I'd run a calibration session across ten PMs with two independent reviewers scoring the same set of artifacts. If inter-rater reliability falls below 80%, the rubric needs tightening before scaling.\n\nWhat I explicitly exclude: features shipped, roadmap completion, and time-in-role-these reward output over impact.\n\nCore trade-off: Outcome metrics are the most credible signal but the slowest. Process metrics are faster but gameable. The combination reduces both false positives and false negatives.",
