    "q": "How do you approach product metrics governance  . ensuring data is trustworthy and decisions are well-grounded?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Framework: Metrics governance requires three pillars - definition rigor, instrumentation reliability, and interpretation discipline.\n\nStart by building a metrics hierarchy: North Star → 3-5 L1 outcome metrics → L2 leading indicators → diagnostic signals. This prevents metric proliferation and creates clear accountability when numbers move.\n\nData validation before any decision: Every core metric needs a documented definition (what counts as \"active user\"?), an owner, and a versioned changelog. When instrumentation changes, I run a parallel measurement period - old and new definitions simultaneously - to quantify the delta before cutting over. This protects historical comparability.\n\nPilot approach: For new metrics, I run a 2-week instrumentation audit with engineering: independently reimplement the tracking, compare outputs, and surface discrepancies. I've consistently found 10-20% variance in \"simple\" events like checkout completions due to edge cases.\n\nSuccess metrics for governance itself:\n- Decision reversal rate due to data errors (target: <5%)\n- Metric definition coverage (100% of L1 metrics documented)\n- Time to detect instrumentation breakage (<48 hours)\n\nTrade-offs I'm explicit about: Governance creates overhead. Strict change protocols slow feature velocity. The calibration point is proportional rigor - high-stakes growth or monetization metrics warrant quarterly audits; exploratory product metrics don't. Applying equal scrutiny everywhere kills speed without proportional accuracy gains.\n\nThe failure mode I prevent most is HiPPO-driven metric selection - choosing definitions after seeing numbers that support a predetermined conclusion.",
