    "q": "What is your approach to competitive intelligence as a product organization practice?",
    "subcategory": "product_strategy",
    "a": "Competitive intelligence should inform strategy validation, not roadmap generation. Here's the framework I use to make it actionable rather than decorative.\n\nStructure around decisions, not monitoring. The only useful question is: \"Why did we win or lose, and what role did competitive differentiation play?\" Not \"what did they ship.\" Every significant deal outcome gets logged against a structured template: competitor, deciding factor, unmet user need. Sales is the primary input, but the analysis must be systematic.\n\nValidate with data before acting. Before any competitive insight influences prioritization, I require corroboration from at least two sources-win/loss interviews, churn surveys, support tickets, or usage data. A single sales rep's anecdote about a competitor feature is noise until confirmed.\n\nPilot before pivoting. If competitive analysis suggests a capability gap, I run a scoped A/B test or a 60-day sales enablement experiment before committing roadmap resources. Metrics: win rate change in competitive deals, deal velocity, and churn rate among at-risk segments.\n\nDistinguish signal from noise explicitly. Competitors ship constantly. Signal is when a move could shift user behavior or redefine market boundaries. Positioning changes-messaging, partnerships, market emphasis-typically precede product shifts by two quarters, so I track those leading indicators.\n\nThe core trade-off: Rigorous competitive intelligence takes real investment. The alternative cost is faster, cheaper, but reactive-you end up building their roadmap instead of yours.",
